/li>\n<li>insects: 54.6</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul></div>\n<p>Researchers Kate Howlett and Edgar Turner asked 401 schoolchildren in the United Kingdom to draw and label local wildlife. The researchers suggest that the children may be more aware of mammals than of other animals in their environments.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice best describes data from the graph that support the researchers&rsquo; conclusion?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"49cb926d-9b0e-49f6-b62d-524fbb75dd4d","content":"<p>There were about as many drawings with an example of an insect as there were with an example of a bird.</p>"},{"id":"bda201c8-1f81-434e-9226-24fa77f99878","content":"<p>The fewest number of drawings contained an example of a bird.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"9c415071-f7fe-4814-8858-2952bfef81ac","content":"<p>More of the drawings had an example of an insect than an example of a mammal.</p>"},{"id":"609f5fec-a230-4425-af4f-6aafc7551810","content":"<p>More of the drawings had an example of a mammal than either an example of a bird or an example of an insect.</p>"}],"keys":["609f5fec-a230-4425-af4f-6aafc7551810"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer because it accurately describes data from the graph that support the researchers&rsquo; conclusion that children may be more aware of mammals than of other animals. The graph presents the percent of drawings by schoolchildren that contained an example of an animal from a certain group (mammals, birds, or insects). The graph shows that approximately 80% of the drawings contained an example of a mammal, while only approximately 69% contained an example of a bird and only about 55% contained an example of an insect. The fact that mammals were included in more drawings than either birds or insects were supports the idea that children may be particularly aware of mammals in their environments.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the graph shows that there were not \"about as many\" drawings with insects as there were drawings with birds (approximately 69% of the drawings included an example of a bird, while only about 55% included an example of an insect). Further, comparing only the numbers for birds and insects wouldn&rsquo;t indicate anything about the schoolchildren&rsquo;s awareness of mammals. Choice B is incorrect because the graph shows that insects, not birds, were the group that appeared in the fewest number of drawings (approximately 69% of the drawings included an example of a bird but only about 55% included an example of an insect). Further, stating that examples from one nonmammal group appeared in the fewest number of drawings would provide no evidence about the schoolchildren&rsquo;s awareness of mammals. Choice C is incorrect because the graph shows that more drawings had an example of a mammal than an example of an insect, not the other way around (approximately 80% of the drawings included an example of a mammal, while only about 55% included an example of an insect). Further, examples of a nonmammal group appearing in more drawings than examples of mammals did would actually weaken the claim that schoolchildren may be more aware of mammals than of other animals.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1776362771169,"updateDate":1776362771169}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1776362771169, 1776362771169),
    ($SATQ$0b696a0c$SATQ$, $SATQ$b6c6791d-a741-4cf2-a375-015d06958e65$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$e283424e-fa7e-4a71-8def-dc0184ef9104$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$CID$SATQ$, $SATQ$Central Ideas and Details$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>NASA&rsquo;s <em>Cassini</em> probe has detected an unusual wobble in the rotation of Mimas, Saturn&rsquo;s smallest moon. Using a computer model to study Mimas&rsquo;s gravitational interactions with Saturn and tidal forces, geophysicist Alyssa Rhoden and colleagues have proposed that this wobble could be due to a liquid ocean moving beneath the moon&rsquo;s icy surface. The researchers believe other moons should be examined to see if they too might have oceans hidden beneath their surfaces.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice best states the main idea of the text?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"47fa43e5-9de0-421b-97cb-3fc70a178699","content":"<p>Rhoden and colleagues were the first to confirm that several of Saturn&rsquo;s moons contain hidden oceans.</p>"},{"id":"12741d28-9263-4e10-8c43-e56705b4b69c","content":"<p>Research has failed to identify signs that there is an ocean hidden beneath the surface of Mimas.</p>"},{"id":"0dce3f23-856b-4173-b925-b2d3396e5fc5","content":"<p>Rhoden and colleagues created a new computer model that identifies moons with hidden oceans without needing to analyze the moons&rsquo; rotation.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"c1d7ad12-0e0b-4feb-b3d2-1d98e7c2e43c","content":"<p>Research has revealed that an oddity in the rotation of Mimas could be explained by an ocean hidden beneath its surface.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["c1d7ad12-0e0b-4feb-b3d2-1d98e7c2e43c"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. The study isn&rsquo;t definitive, but it says that Mimas&rsquo;s wobbly rotation could be explained by the hidden ocean. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This choice goes too far beyond the information in the text. Rhoden&rsquo;s team proposed that one moon of Saturn could have a liquid ocean beneath its surface, and that other moons should also be examined, but no one has confirmed anything. Choice B is incorrect. This choice conflicts with the text. Research has &nbsp;identified at least one sign&mdash;the unusual wobble in Mimas&rsquo;s rotation&mdash;that might be due to a hidden ocean beneath its surface.&nbsp;Choice C is incorrect. This choice doesn&rsquo;t reflect the text. The computer model studies &ldquo;gravitational interactions,&rdquo; which seem to account for the wobbly rotation of Mimas. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-CID-E004$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$c7599bd5-f988-473b-8b0b-cdc79574af8a$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"0b696a0c","external_id":"b6c6791d-a741-4cf2-a375-015d06958e65","vaultid":"e283424e-fa7e-4a71-8def-dc0184ef9104","uId":"b52d3e4d-d063-4f3b-b9d5-fc5136ad8066","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"CID","skill_desc":"Central Ideas and Details","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":2,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>NASA&rsquo;s <em>Cassini</em> probe has detected an unusual wobble in the rotation of Mimas, Saturn&rsquo;s smallest moon. Using a computer model to study Mimas&rsquo;s gravitational interactions with Saturn and tidal forces, geophysicist Alyssa Rhoden and colleagues have proposed that this wobble could be due to a liquid ocean moving beneath the moon&rsquo;s icy surface. The researchers believe other moons should be examined to see if they too might have oceans hidden beneath their surfaces.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice best states the main idea of the text?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"47fa43e5-9de0-421b-97cb-3fc70a178699","content":"<p>Rhoden and colleagues were the first to confirm that several of Saturn&rsquo;s moons contain hidden oceans.</p>"},{"id":"12741d28-9263-4e10-8c43-e56705b4b69c","content":"<p>Research has failed to identify signs that there is an ocean hidden beneath the surface of Mimas.</p>"},{"id":"0dce3f23-856b-4173-b925-b2d3396e5fc5","content":"<p>Rhoden and colleagues created a new computer model that identifies moons with hidden oceans without needing to analyze the moons&rsquo; rotation.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"c1d7ad12-0e0b-4feb-b3d2-1d98e7c2e43c","content":"<p>Research has revealed that an oddity in the rotation of Mimas could be explained by an ocean hidden beneath its surface.</p>"}],"keys":["c1d7ad12-0e0b-4feb-b3d2-1d98e7c2e43c"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer. The study isn&rsquo;t definitive, but it says that Mimas&rsquo;s wobbly rotation could be explained by the hidden ocean. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This choice goes too far beyond the information in the text. Rhoden&rsquo;s team proposed that one moon of Saturn could have a liquid ocean beneath its surface, and that other moons should also be examined, but no one has confirmed anything. Choice B is incorrect. This choice conflicts with the text. Research has &nbsp;identified at least one sign&mdash;the unusual wobble in Mimas&rsquo;s rotation&mdash;that might be due to a hidden ocean beneath its surface.&nbsp;Choice C is incorrect. This choice doesn&rsquo;t reflect the text. The computer model studies &ldquo;gravitational interactions,&rdquo; which seem to account for the wobbly rotation of Mimas. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-CID-E004","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"c7599bd5-f988-473b-8b0b-cdc79574af8a","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959359,"updateDate":1691007959359}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959359, 1691007959359),
    ($SATQ$0b96fa93$SATQ$, $SATQ$0a101d3f-287a-4888-8521-b280f7b9fb5e$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$7b342941-df67-4729-82c9-919b4f6b419a$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$COE$SATQ$, $SATQ$Command of Evidence$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p><figure class="table"><table class="gdr"><caption style="caption-side: top;"><p style="text-align: center;">Maximum Height of Maple Trees When Fully Grown</p></caption><thead><tr><th scope="col" style="text-align: center; vertical-align: bottom;">Tree type</th><th scope="col" style="text-align: center; vertical-align: bottom;">Maximum height (feet)</th><th scope="col" style="text-align: center; vertical-align: bottom;">Native to North America</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row" style="text-align: left;">Sugar maple</th><td style="text-align: center;">75</td><td>yes</td></tr><tr><th scope="row" style="text-align: left;">Silver maple</th><td style="text-align: center;">70</td><td>yes</td></tr><tr><th scope="row" style="text-align: left;">Red maple</th><td style="text-align: center;">60</td><td>yes</td></tr><tr><th scope="row" style="text-align: left;">Japanese maple</th><td style="text-align: center;">25</td><td>no</td></tr><tr><th scope="row" style="text-align: left;">Norway maple</th><td style="text-align: center;">50</td><td>no</td></tr></tbody></table></figure></p>
<p>For a school project, a forestry student needs to recommend a maple tree that is native to North America and won&rsquo;t grow more than 60 feet in height. Based on the characteristics of five common maple trees, she has decided to select a <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> </p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the text?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"3265ec1e-9dee-4f24-a16f-c61f587f7132","content":"<p>silver maple.</p>"},{"id":"46f027ba-b5a4-4529-b824-8bbb36be44ff","content":"<p>sugar maple.</p>"},{"id":"51e78b2a-4f75-4a32-9884-92a5c9797a6c","content":"<p>red maple.</p>"},{"id":"8da758e4-32d6-414f-b491-ad82b9e71c1e","content":"<p>Norway maple.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["51e78b2a-4f75-4a32-9884-92a5c9797a6c"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer because it most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement about the forestry student&rsquo;s project. The table shows five types of maple trees, each tree&rsquo;s maximum height, and whether each tree is native to North America. The text indicates that the student needs to recommend a maple tree that&rsquo;s native to North America and won&rsquo;t reach a height greater than 60 feet. The red maple is the only tree listed in the table that meets these criteria: its maximum height is 60 feet&mdash;meaning that it won&rsquo;t grow higher than 60 feet&mdash;and it&rsquo;s native to North America. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the text states that the student needs to recommend a tree that&rsquo;s native to North America and won&rsquo;t grow higher than 60 feet, but the table shows that the maximum height of the silver maple is 70 feet.&nbsp;Choice B is incorrect because the text states that the student needs to recommend a tree that&rsquo;s native to North America and won&rsquo;t grow higher than 60 feet, but the table shows that the maximum height of the sugar maple is 75 feet.&nbsp;Choice D is incorrect because the text states that the student needs to recommend a tree that&rsquo;s native to North America and won&rsquo;t grow higher than 60 feet, but the table shows that the Norway maple isn&rsquo;t native to North America.&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-523-QUA$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$bea36b1f-91ce-4260-bab9-cd47f76991bc$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"0b96fa93","external_id":"0a101d3f-287a-4888-8521-b280f7b9fb5e","vaultid":"7b342941-df67-4729-82c9-919b4f6b419a","uId":"b2366221-d8f9-475d-b091-c04111c7fed5","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"COE","skill_desc":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p><figure class=\"table\"><table class=\"gdr\"><caption style=\"caption-side: top;\"><p style=\"text-align: center;\">Maximum Height of Maple Trees When Fully Grown</p></caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\" style=\"text-align: center; vertical-align: bottom;\">Tree type</th><th scope=\"col\" style=\"text-align: center; vertical-align: bottom;\">Maximum height (feet)</th><th scope=\"col\" style=\"text-align: center; vertical-align: bottom;\">Native to North America</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\" style=\"text-align: left;\">Sugar maple</th><td style=\"text-align: center;\">75</td><td>yes</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\" style=\"text-align: left;\">Silver maple</th><td style=\"text-align: center;\">70</td><td>yes</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\" style=\"text-align: left;\">Red maple</th><td style=\"text-align: center;\">60</td><td>yes</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\" style=\"text-align: left;\">Japanese maple</th><td style=\"text-align: center;\">25</td><td>no</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\" style=\"text-align: left;\">Norway maple</th><td style=\"text-align: center;\">50</td><td>no</td></tr></tbody></table></figure></p>\n<p>For a school project, a forestry student needs to recommend a maple tree that is native to North America and won&rsquo;t grow more than 60 feet in height. Based on the characteristics of five common maple trees, she has decided to select a <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> </p>","stem":"<p>Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the text?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"3265ec1e-9dee-4f24-a16f-c61f587f7132","content":"<p>silver maple.</p>"},{"id":"46f027ba-b5a4-4529-b824-8bbb36be44ff","content":"<p>sugar maple.</p>"},{"id":"51e78b2a-4f75-4a32-9884-92a5c9797a6c","content":"<p>red maple.</p>"},{"id":"8da758e4-32d6-414f-b491-ad82b9e71c1e","content":"<p>Norway maple.</p>"}],"keys":["51e78b2a-4f75-4a32-9884-92a5c9797a6c"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer because it most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement about the forestry student&rsquo;s project. The table shows five types of maple trees, each tree&rsquo;s maximum height, and whether each tree is native to North America. The text indicates that the student needs to recommend a maple tree that&rsquo;s native to North America and won&rsquo;t reach a height greater than 60 feet. The red maple is the only tree listed in the table that meets these criteria: its maximum height is 60 feet&mdash;meaning that it won&rsquo;t grow higher than 60 feet&mdash;and it&rsquo;s native to North America. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the text states that the student needs to recommend a tree that&rsquo;s native to North America and won&rsquo;t grow higher than 60 feet, but the table shows that the maximum height of the silver maple is 70 feet.&nbsp;Choice B is incorrect because the text states that the student needs to recommend a tree that&rsquo;s native to North America and won&rsquo;t grow higher than 60 feet, but the table shows that the maximum height of the sugar maple is 75 feet.&nbsp;Choice D is incorrect because the text states that the student needs to recommend a tree that&rsquo;s native to North America and won&rsquo;t grow higher than 60 feet, but the table shows that the Norway maple isn&rsquo;t native to North America.&nbsp;</p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-523-QUA","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"bea36b1f-91ce-4260-bab9-cd47f76991bc","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959337,"updateDate":1691007959337}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959337, 1691007959337),
    ($SATQ$0c622cfb$SATQ$, $SATQ$a7544373-0f0e-48ad-bf64-b636aa0c867e$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$46fe6879-e39d-4674-8285-0281c7000244$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$COE$SATQ$, $SATQ$Command of Evidence$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Although it&rsquo;s clear that Mars once had liquid water on its surface, astronomers have debated whether the evidence of ancient water reflects a prolonged phase of warm, wet conditions&mdash;the so-called wet and warm scenario&mdash;or a brief period of melting in an otherwise consistently frozen environment. Researchers Benjamin T. Cardenas and Michael P. Lamb recently added to this debate by using data from NASA and the Mars Orbiter Laser Altimeter to map the topography of what is now a large basin in Mars&rsquo;s northern hemisphere. Cardenas and Lamb concluded that the wet and warm scenario is likely correct.&nbsp;&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which finding about the basin, if true, would most directly support Cardenas and Lamb&rsquo;s conclusion?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"64a55b65-d712-4f4a-addb-fbf1d4ce05e3","content":"<p>Its dimensions and shape indicate that it is unlikely to have formed as the result of an asteroid or comet impact.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"2d8e62e9-4ca6-4fc0-8504-fc0f6e6d89d6","content":"<p>It has features suggesting that it once held an ocean that underwent gradual sea-level changes over an extended time.</p>"},{"id":"c5cce04f-c527-4b49-80fa-d4687dce21de","content":"<p>Its physical characteristics are most consistent with it having formed as a result of a massive but short-lived influx of liquid water.</p>"},{"id":"3fc94333-6d5d-43ce-8ff1-58117f4c987a","content":"<p>It is surrounded by channels that could have been formed either by running water or by flowing lava.&nbsp;</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["2d8e62e9-4ca6-4fc0-8504-fc0f6e6d89d6"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer. This choice provides direct support for the researchers&rsquo; conclusion. If the basin once held an ocean of liquid water for "an extended time," that supports the "wet and warm scenario" on Mars, which involved a "prolonged" period of temperate conditions (and not just a "brief period of melting").</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This choice doesn&rsquo;t provide support for the researchers&rsquo; conclusions. The dimensions of the basin have little bearing on whether or not it was filled with liquid water, and for how long, and that&rsquo;s the evidence that would support the "wet and warm" theory. Similarly, whether or not the basin was formed by an asteroid or a comet is irrelevant to the question of whether or not there was water in the basin for a long period. Choice C is incorrect. This choice does not provide support for the researchers&rsquo; conclusions, but rather the opposite. A "massive but short lived influx" of liquid water is not the same as a "prolonged phase of warm, wet conditions." It more reflects the opposing theory, a "brief period of melting." Choice D is incorrect. This choice does not support Cardenas and Lamb&rsquo;s conclusion. Both theories about liquid water on Mars ("wet and warm" and "brief period of melting") involve flowing water, but lava isn&rsquo;t mentioned at all in the text. Therefore, this choice doesn&rsquo;t provide exclusive support for either theory.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-TXT-M010$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$eeb37224-9bbc-4504-95a0-0fd596bf9c2c$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"0c622cfb","external_id":"a7544373-0f0e-48ad-bf64-b636aa0c867e","vaultid":"46fe6879-e39d-4674-8285-0281c7000244","uId":"2eaffe66-66a1-4561-9009-3150c3ff7212","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"COE","skill_desc":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Although it&rsquo;s clear that Mars once had liquid water on its surface, astronomers have debated whether the evidence of ancient water reflects a prolonged phase of warm, wet conditions&mdash;the so-called wet and warm scenario&mdash;or a brief period of melting in an otherwise consistently frozen environment. Researchers Benjamin T. Cardenas and Michael P. Lamb recently added to this debate by using data from NASA and the Mars Orbiter Laser Altimeter to map the topography of what is now a large basin in Mars&rsquo;s northern hemisphere. Cardenas and Lamb concluded that the wet and warm scenario is likely correct.&nbsp;&nbsp;</p>","stem":"<p>Which finding about the basin, if true, would most directly support Cardenas and Lamb&rsquo;s conclusion?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"64a55b65-d712-4f4a-addb-fbf1d4ce05e3","content":"<p>Its dimensions and shape indicate that it is unlikely to have formed as the result of an asteroid or comet impact.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"2d8e62e9-4ca6-4fc0-8504-fc0f6e6d89d6","content":"<p>It has features suggesting that it once held an ocean that underwent gradual sea-level changes over an extended time.</p>"},{"id":"c5cce04f-c527-4b49-80fa-d4687dce21de","content":"<p>Its physical characteristics are most consistent with it having formed as a result of a massive but short-lived influx of liquid water.</p>"},{"id":"3fc94333-6d5d-43ce-8ff1-58117f4c987a","content":"<p>It is surrounded by channels that could have been formed either by running water or by flowing lava.&nbsp;</p>"}],"keys":["2d8e62e9-4ca6-4fc0-8504-fc0f6e6d89d6"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer. This choice provides direct support for the researchers&rsquo; conclusion. If the basin once held an ocean of liquid water for \"an extended time,\" that supports the \"wet and warm scenario\" on Mars, which involved a \"prolonged\" period of temperate conditions (and not just a \"brief period of melting\").</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This choice doesn&rsquo;t provide support for the researchers&rsquo; conclusions. The dimensions of the basin have little bearing on whether or not it was filled with liquid water, and for how long, and that&rsquo;s the evidence that would support the \"wet and warm\" theory. Similarly, whether or not the basin was formed by an asteroid or a comet is irrelevant to the question of whether or not there was water in the basin for a long period. Choice C is incorrect. This choice does not provide support for the researchers&rsquo; conclusions, but rather the opposite. A \"massive but short lived influx\" of liquid water is not the same as a \"prolonged phase of warm, wet conditions.\" It more reflects the opposing theory, a \"brief period of melting.\" Choice D is incorrect. This choice does not support Cardenas and Lamb&rsquo;s conclusion. Both theories about liquid water on Mars (\"wet and warm\" and \"brief period of melting\") involve flowing water, but lava isn&rsquo;t mentioned at all in the text. Therefore, this choice doesn&rsquo;t provide exclusive support for either theory.</p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-TXT-M010","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"eeb37224-9bbc-4504-95a0-0fd596bf9c2c","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959377,"updateDate":1691007959377}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959377, 1691007959377),
    ($SATQ$0cf13ece$SATQ$, $SATQ$f430010d-dd34-405f-b7a8-b70d74e17b0e$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$COE$SATQ$, $SATQ$Command of Evidence$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p><figure class="table"><table class="gdr"><caption style="caption-side: top;"><p style="text-align: center;">Recordings of Female Bottlenose Dolphins with Their Calves</p></caption><thead><tr><th scope="col" style="text-align: center;vertical-align: bottom;">Dolphin ID</th><th scope="col" style="text-align: center;vertical-align: bottom;">Recording year</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row" style="text-align: left;">FB07</th><td style="text-align: center;">2012</td></tr><tr><th scope="row" style="text-align: left;">FB25</th><td style="text-align: center;">1989</td></tr><tr><th scope="row" style="text-align: left;">FB43</th><td style="text-align: center;">1992</td></tr><tr><th scope="row" style="text-align: left;">FB79</th><td style="text-align: center;">2018</td></tr></tbody></table></figure></p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>In a study of bottlenose dolphins, biologist Laela <span data-ssml-sub-alias="S">S.</span> Sayigh and a team of researchers analyzed recordings of female bottlenose dolphins interacting with their calves.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>According to the table, in which year was the dolphin with the ID FB43 recorded with her calf?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"5dc8541e-92fb-4bdb-81ae-79b10e9c5103","content":"<p>1999</p>"},{"id":"af8b74be-ce59-47e7-af8c-8759062daae7","content":"<p>2012</p>"},{"id":"3c4cc6b2-1526-4626-ac0c-f3752a31ee75","content":"<p>2020</p>"},{"id":"03c6aa6d-abf6-409a-8058-1aff3a90b40a","content":"<p>1992</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["03c6aa6d-abf6-409a-8058-1aff3a90b40a"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer because it accurately reflects the data in the table. According to the table, the dolphin with ID FB43 has a recording year of 1992.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. None of the dolphins in the table have a recording year of 1999. Choice B is incorrect. The table shows 2012 as the recording year for the dolphin with ID FB07, not ID FB43. Choice C is incorrect. None of the dolphins in the table have a recording year of 2020.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"0cf13ece","external_id":"f430010d-dd34-405f-b7a8-b70d74e17b0e","vaultid":null,"uId":"316d36da-9bfe-41b0-82e1-9a023d1089f5","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"COE","skill_desc":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":1,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p><figure class=\"table\"><table class=\"gdr\"><caption style=\"caption-side: top;\"><p style=\"text-align: center;\">Recordings of Female Bottlenose Dolphins with Their Calves</p></caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\" style=\"text-align: center;vertical-align: bottom;\">Dolphin ID</th><th scope=\"col\" style=\"text-align: center;vertical-align: bottom;\">Recording year</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\" style=\"text-align: left;\">FB07</th><td style=\"text-align: center;\">2012</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\" style=\"text-align: left;\">FB25</th><td style=\"text-align: center;\">1989</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\" style=\"text-align: left;\">FB43</th><td style=\"text-align: center;\">1992</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\" style=\"text-align: left;\">FB79</th><td style=\"text-align: center;\">2018</td></tr></tbody></table></figure></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p>In a study of bottlenose dolphins, biologist Laela <span data-ssml-sub-alias=\"S\">S.</span> Sayigh and a team of researchers analyzed recordings of female bottlenose dolphins interacting with their calves.</p>","stem":"<p>According to the table, in which year was the dolphin with the ID FB43 recorded with her calf?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"5dc8541e-92fb-4bdb-81ae-79b10e9c5103","content":"<p>1999</p>"},{"id":"af8b74be-ce59-47e7-af8c-8759062daae7","content":"<p>2012</p>"},{"id":"3c4cc6b2-1526-4626-ac0c-f3752a31ee75","content":"<p>2020</p>"},{"id":"03c6aa6d-abf6-409a-8058-1aff3a90b40a","content":"<p>1992</p>"}],"keys":["03c6aa6d-abf6-409a-8058-1aff3a90b40a"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer because it accurately reflects the data in the table. According to the table, the dolphin with ID FB43 has a recording year of 1992.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. None of the dolphins in the table have a recording year of 1999. Choice B is incorrect. The table shows 2012 as the recording year for the dolphin with ID FB07, not ID FB43. Choice C is incorrect. None of the dolphins in the table have a recording year of 2020.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1743430669215,"updateDate":1743430669215}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1743430669215, 1743430669215),
    ($SATQ$0d7f4966$SATQ$, $SATQ$a34fe949-4c0c-463d-a566-a28ea8069426$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$COE$SATQ$, $SATQ$Command of Evidence$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Jean-Bernard Caron and colleagues recently discovered a cache of jellyfish fossils in the Burgess Shale, a site in the Canadian Rockies that is rich in fossils from the Cambrian period (over 500 million years ago). Caron and colleagues claim that these are the oldest jellyfish fossils ever discovered. In the past twenty years, two sites in China and the United States have yielded fossils of a similar age that some experts believe are most likely jellyfish due to their shapes and the appearance of projecting tentacles. But Caron and colleagues argue that the apparent tentacles are in fact the comb rows of ctenophores, gelatinous animals that are only distantly related to jellyfish.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which statement, if true, would most directly weaken the claim by Caron and colleagues about the fossils found in China and the United States?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"1d127356-9676-4942-a245-046c2ca7a341","content":"<p>Sites in the Canadian Rockies from later periods than the Cambrian period have yielded fossils that have been conclusively identified as ctenophore fossils.</p>"},{"id":"0d311029-0e52-4b8a-9349-eb97e7c157ae","content":"<p>The fossils found in China and the United States are so poorly preserved that though they cannot be conclusively identified as jellyfish, they cannot be conclusively identified as ctenophores either.</p>"},{"id":"b479319d-de49-45fd-a972-cbc41508b847","content":"<p>While ctenophore fossils have been discovered in China and the United States, they have never been discovered in the Burgess Shale.</p>"},{"id":"a64e5b1a-18b5-4d46-8c02-d87a7ea353b0","content":"<p>The fossils discovered by Caron and colleagues in the Burgess Shale were better preserved than the fossils discovered by other researchers in China and the United States.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["0d311029-0e52-4b8a-9349-eb97e7c157ae"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer because it presents a statement that, if true, would most directly weaken Caron and colleagues&rsquo; claim that the apparent tentacles in the Chinese and American fossils are actually ctenophore comb rows. If the fossils are so poorly preserved that they cannot be conclusively identified as either organism, neither the claim that they are jellyfish nor, as Caron claims, that they are ctenophores would be supported.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. Caron&rsquo;s claim is that fossils from the US and China are ctenophores, not jellyfish. These fossils are said to be "of a similar age" to the Cambrian fossils found in the Canadian Rockies. And nothing in the text or this choice suggests that the presence or absence of ctenophores after the Cambrian would have any bearing on whether the Cambrian fossils from the US and China are ctenophores. Choice C is incorrect. Caron&rsquo;s claim is that fossils from the US and China are ctenophores, not jellyfish. Nothing in the text suggests that the presence or absence of ctenophores in the Burgess Shale (in Canada) would affect whether the fossils found in the US and China are ctenophores. Choice D is incorrect. Caron&rsquo;s claim is that fossils from the US and China are ctenophores, not jellyfish. Although fossil quality is a plausible issue for the research described in the text, nothing in the text or this choice suggests that the fossils from US and China would have been too poorly preserved for proper identification.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"0d7f4966","external_id":"a34fe949-4c0c-463d-a566-a28ea8069426","vaultid":null,"uId":"078bcab7-724e-4781-9463-dc1c0eeb845b","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"COE","skill_desc":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Jean-Bernard Caron and colleagues recently discovered a cache of jellyfish fossils in the Burgess Shale, a site in the Canadian Rockies that is rich in fossils from the Cambrian period (over 500 million years ago). Caron and colleagues claim that these are the oldest jellyfish fossils ever discovered. In the past twenty years, two sites in China and the United States have yielded fossils of a similar age that some experts believe are most likely jellyfish due to their shapes and the appearance of projecting tentacles. But Caron and colleagues argue that the apparent tentacles are in fact the comb rows of ctenophores, gelatinous animals that are only distantly related to jellyfish.</p>","stem":"<p>Which statement, if true, would most directly weaken the claim by Caron and colleagues about the fossils found in China and the United States?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"1d127356-9676-4942-a245-046c2ca7a341","content":"<p>Sites in the Canadian Rockies from later periods than the Cambrian period have yielded fossils that have been conclusively identified as ctenophore fossils.</p>"},{"id":"0d311029-0e52-4b8a-9349-eb97e7c157ae","content":"<p>The fossils found in China and the United States are so poorly preserved that though they cannot be conclusively identified as jellyfish, they cannot be conclusively identified as ctenophores either.</p>"},{"id":"b479319d-de49-45fd-a972-cbc41508b847","content":"<p>While ctenophore fossils have been discovered in China and the United States, they have never been discovered in the Burgess Shale.</p>"},{"id":"a64e5b1a-18b5-4d46-8c02-d87a7ea353b0","content":"<p>The fossils discovered by Caron and colleagues in the Burgess Shale were better preserved than the fossils discovered by other researchers in China and the United States.</p>"}],"keys":["0d311029-0e52-4b8a-9349-eb97e7c157ae"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer because it presents a statement that, if true, would most directly weaken Caron and colleagues&rsquo; claim that the apparent tentacles in the Chinese and American fossils are actually ctenophore comb rows. If the fossils are so poorly preserved that they cannot be conclusively identified as either organism, neither the claim that they are jellyfish nor, as Caron claims, that they are ctenophores would be supported.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. Caron&rsquo;s claim is that fossils from the US and China are ctenophores, not jellyfish. These fossils are said to be \"of a similar age\" to the Cambrian fossils found in the Canadian Rockies. And nothing in the text or this choice suggests that the presence or absence of ctenophores after the Cambrian would have any bearing on whether the Cambrian fossils from the US and China are ctenophores. Choice C is incorrect. Caron&rsquo;s claim is that fossils from the US and China are ctenophores, not jellyfish. Nothing in the text suggests that the presence or absence of ctenophores in the Burgess Shale (in Canada) would affect whether the fossils found in the US and China are ctenophores. Choice D is incorrect. Caron&rsquo;s claim is that fossils from the US and China are ctenophores, not jellyfish. Although fossil quality is a plausible issue for the research described in the text, nothing in the text or this choice suggests that the fossils from US and China would have been too poorly preserved for proper identification.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1755117061190,"updateDate":1755117061190}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1755117061190, 1755117061190),
    ($SATQ$0d81b7d9$SATQ$, $SATQ$b280ba71-e914-4119-8335-a7c8eb92768c$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$CID$SATQ$, $SATQ$Central Ideas and Details$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Paleontologist Alan Tennyson and colleagues studied fossil bones found in New Zealand that are more than 55 million years old. The researchers determined that the fossil bones belonged to two previously unknown species of prehistoric penguins. Moreover, they estimated that one of the two penguin species was more than three times the size of the emperor penguin, which is the largest penguin species that exists today.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Based on the text, which choice best describes the two previously unknown penguin species?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"88d2c5db-2b00-4d2f-aa09-23614ac95b78","content":"<p>They are frequently studied by paleontologists.</p>"},{"id":"60955149-b05a-4189-b49d-f34898e81b40","content":"<p>They are no longer living species.</p>"},{"id":"bc1c79db-1fbf-46eb-ac86-43f3203b4be0","content":"<p>They were smaller than penguin species that exist today.</p>"},{"id":"b484b101-0f9a-4e83-835f-e366143cca15","content":"<p>They spent little of their lives in water.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["60955149-b05a-4189-b49d-f34898e81b40"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer because it presents a statement about the two previously unknown penguin species that is supported by the text. According to the text, Alan Tennyson and colleagues studied fossil bones belonging to two previously unknown penguin species that are described as prehistoric, or ancient. The text goes on to say that one of the two species was considerably larger than the emperor penguin, which is currently the largest penguin in existence. Thus, the text indicates that the other species aren&rsquo;t in existence today, or that the two previously unknown penguin species are no longer living species.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. Although the text states that Alan Tennyson is a paleontologist who studied the fossils of two previously unknown penguin species, it doesn&rsquo;t say whether these penguins are frequently studied by paleontologists. Choice C is incorrect because the text says that one of the two previously unknown penguin species was three times larger than the emperor penguin (a penguin alive today), not that they were both smaller than penguin species that exist today. Choice D is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t discuss whether the two previously unknown penguin species spent any time in water.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"0d81b7d9","external_id":"b280ba71-e914-4119-8335-a7c8eb92768c","vaultid":null,"uId":"1d12e318-d7ff-40c6-ac7d-0b861646649f","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"CID","skill_desc":"Central Ideas and Details","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Paleontologist Alan Tennyson and colleagues studied fossil bones found in New Zealand that are more than 55 million years old. The researchers determined that the fossil bones belonged to two previously unknown species of prehistoric penguins. Moreover, they estimated that one of the two penguin species was more than three times the size of the emperor penguin, which is the largest penguin species that exists today.</p>","stem":"<p>Based on the text, which choice best describes the two previously unknown penguin species?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"88d2c5db-2b00-4d2f-aa09-23614ac95b78","content":"<p>They are frequently studied by paleontologists.</p>"},{"id":"60955149-b05a-4189-b49d-f34898e81b40","content":"<p>They are no longer living species.</p>"},{"id":"bc1c79db-1fbf-46eb-ac86-43f3203b4be0","content":"<p>They were smaller than penguin species that exist today.</p>"},{"id":"b484b101-0f9a-4e83-835f-e366143cca15","content":"<p>They spent little of their lives in water.</p>"}],"keys":["60955149-b05a-4189-b49d-f34898e81b40"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer because it presents a statement about the two previously unknown penguin species that is supported by the text. According to the text, Alan Tennyson and colleagues studied fossil bones belonging to two previously unknown penguin species that are described as prehistoric, or ancient. The text goes on to say that one of the two species was considerably larger than the emperor penguin, which is currently the largest penguin in existence. Thus, the text indicates that the other species aren&rsquo;t in existence today, or that the two previously unknown penguin species are no longer living species.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. Although the text states that Alan Tennyson is a paleontologist who studied the fossils of two previously unknown penguin species, it doesn&rsquo;t say whether these penguins are frequently studied by paleontologists. Choice C is incorrect because the text says that one of the two previously unknown penguin species was three times larger than the emperor penguin (a penguin alive today), not that they were both smaller than penguin species that exist today. Choice D is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t discuss whether the two previously unknown penguin species spent any time in water.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323039,"updateDate":1730147323039}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323039, 1730147323039),
    ($SATQ$0dba14e6$SATQ$, $SATQ$e6dbeac0-5d8f-4b95-88aa-f9a3718cc8b4$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$b3998720-da59-4c8d-a1a1-d4a4a1a0cbbf$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$INF$SATQ$, $SATQ$Inferences$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The increased integration of digital technologies throughout the process of book creation in the late 20th and early 21st centuries lowered the costs of book production, but those decreased costs have been most significant in the manufacturing and distribution process, which occurs after the authoring, editing, and design of the book are complete. This suggests that in the late 20th and early 21st centuries, <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span></p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice most logically completes the text?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"9bd25604-1b0f-44ce-92a5-77f91d2b6458","content":"<p>digital technologies made it easier than it had been previously for authors to write very long works and get them published.</p>"},{"id":"f39c773d-a8fc-4fe4-b20c-81c1fbbd3bbd","content":"<p>customers generally expected the cost of books to decline relative to the cost of other consumer goods.</p>"},{"id":"2aa53fbf-d922-4388-885c-37ce2216f91e","content":"<p>publishers increased the variety of their offerings by printing more unique titles but also printed fewer copies of each title.</p>"},{"id":"c30c9648-ef51-4211-afaf-facc5382c8aa","content":"<p>the costs of writing, editing, and designing a book were less affected by the technologies used than were the costs of manufacturing and distributing a book.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["c30c9648-ef51-4211-afaf-facc5382c8aa"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer because it presents the conclusion that most logically follows from the text&rsquo;s discussion of how digital technologies affected the process of book creation. The text explains that in the late 20th and early 21st centuries digital technologies lowered book production costs most significantly in manufacturing and distribution. The text goes on to point out that authoring, editing, and book design are distinct steps in the process that occur before manufacturing and distribution. Because the savings connected to digital technologies have been most significant in manufacturing and distribution, it&rsquo;s reasonable to infer that those technologies had less of an effect on writing, editing, and designing books.&nbsp;</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the text focuses on lowered book production costs that occur after authoring has taken place; there&rsquo;s no indication in the text whether digital technologies made writing and publishing lengthy books easier.&nbsp;Choice B is incorrect. Although it&rsquo;s logical to conclude that customers would expect the cost of books to decline if production costs have declined, the text doesn&rsquo;t address customer expectations for the cost of books or any other consumer goods.&nbsp;Choice C is incorrect because the text focuses broadly on how digital technologies have affected the cost of the publishing process; it doesn&rsquo;t address the kinds of books being published or how many copies are printed. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-015-INF$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$c6d312d1-9b68-4c5e-aa55-1e14bbba425b$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"0dba14e6","external_id":"e6dbeac0-5d8f-4b95-88aa-f9a3718cc8b4","vaultid":"b3998720-da59-4c8d-a1a1-d4a4a1a0cbbf","uId":"7d3d2890-811e-4a94-ab4e-74ad3597587c","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"INF","skill_desc":"Inferences","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The increased integration of digital technologies throughout the process of book creation in the late 20th and early 21st centuries lowered the costs of book production, but those decreased costs have been most significant in the manufacturing and distribution process, which occurs after the authoring, editing, and design of the book are complete. This suggests that in the late 20th and early 21st centuries, <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span></p>","stem":"<p>Which choice most logically completes the text?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"9bd25604-1b0f-44ce-92a5-77f91d2b6458","content":"<p>digital technologies made it easier than it had been previously for authors to write very long works and get them published.</p>"},{"id":"f39c773d-a8fc-4fe4-b20c-81c1fbbd3bbd","content":"<p>customers generally expected the cost of books to decline relative to the cost of other consumer goods.</p>"},{"id":"2aa53fbf-d922-4388-885c-37ce2216f91e","content":"<p>publishers increased the variety of their offerings by printing more unique titles but also printed fewer copies of each title.</p>"},{"id":"c30c9648-ef51-4211-afaf-facc5382c8aa","content":"<p>the costs of writing, editing, and designing a book were less affected by the technologies used than were the costs of manufacturing and distributing a book.</p>"}],"keys":["c30c9648-ef51-4211-afaf-facc5382c8aa"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer because it presents the conclusion that most logically follows from the text&rsquo;s discussion of how digital technologies affected the process of book creation. The text explains that in the late 20th and early 21st centuries digital technologies lowered book production costs most significantly in manufacturing and distribution. The text goes on to point out that authoring, editing, and book design are distinct steps in the process that occur before manufacturing and distribution. Because the savings connected to digital technologies have been most significant in manufacturing and distribution, it&rsquo;s reasonable to infer that those technologies had less of an effect on writing, editing, and designing books.&nbsp;</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the text focuses on lowered book production costs that occur after authoring has taken place; there&rsquo;s no indication in the text whether digital technologies made writing and publishing lengthy books easier.&nbsp;Choice B is incorrect. Although it&rsquo;s logical to conclude that customers would expect the cost of books to decline if production costs have declined, the text doesn&rsquo;t address customer expectations for the cost of books or any other consumer goods.&nbsp;Choice C is incorrect because the text focuses broadly on how digital technologies have affected the cost of the publishing process; it doesn&rsquo;t address the kinds of books being published or how many copies are printed. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-015-INF","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"c6d312d1-9b68-4c5e-aa55-1e14bbba425b","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959315,"updateDate":1691007959315}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959315, 1691007959315),
    ($SATQ$0dccbf17$SATQ$, $SATQ$10131dc4-382d-4485-a578-62a26d8a2e56$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$a2d7522d-7afa-4d45-9c0c-6014f37a16ed$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$INF$SATQ$, $SATQ$Inferences$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Henry Ossawa Tanner&rsquo;s 1893 painting <em>The Banjo</em> <em>Lesson</em>, which depicts an elderly man teaching a boy to play the banjo, is regarded as a landmark in the history of works by Black artists in the United States. Scholars should be cautious when ascribing political or ideological values to the painting, however: beliefs and assumptions that are commonly held now may have been unfamiliar to Tanner and his contemporaries, and vice versa. Scholars who forget this fact when discussing <em>The Banjo Lesson</em> therefore <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span></p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice most logically completes the text?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"847a310f-f5d3-4b95-a79f-93e41f0f7296","content":"<p>risk judging Tanner&rsquo;s painting by standards that may not be historically appropriate.</p>"},{"id":"8443ee27-0fa4-44c8-911e-311573ded2ab","content":"<p>tend to conflate Tanner&rsquo;s political views with those of his contemporaries.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"a18598d0-7394-419b-ace5-b607ebce9f94","content":"<p>forgo analyzing Tanner&rsquo;s painting in favor of analyzing his political activity.</p>"},{"id":"867ffeb7-997d-4033-86cf-fe773c1a754a","content":"<p>wrongly assume that Tanner&rsquo;s painting was intended as a critique of his fellow artists.&nbsp;</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["847a310f-f5d3-4b95-a79f-93e41f0f7296"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer. It most logically completes the text. The text argues that Tanner and his contemporaries may have been unfamiliar with modern beliefs and values. This suggests that scholars who attribute those modern values to Tanner&rsquo;s painting are risking judging the painting by standards that are not historically accurate.&nbsp;&nbsp;</p><p>Choice B is incorrect. It doesn&rsquo;t logically complete the text. The text argues that Tanner AND his contemporaries may have been unfamiliar with modern views. It never suggests that Tanner&rsquo;s views were different from his contemporaries&rsquo; views. &nbsp;Choice C is incorrect. It doesn&rsquo;t logically complete the text. The text never suggests that scholars should analyze Tanner&rsquo;s political activity instead of his painting. Rather, the text argues that Tanner and his contemporaries may have been unfamiliar with modern beliefs and values.&nbsp;Choice D is incorrect. It doesn&rsquo;t logically complete the text. The text never suggests that Tanner wanted to critique his contemporaries with his painting. Rather, the text argues that Tanner AND his contemporaries may have been unfamiliar with modern beliefs and values.&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-005-INF$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$1cc54c82-ef86-42e7-b69c-58b77ef7b8b1$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"0dccbf17","external_id":"10131dc4-382d-4485-a578-62a26d8a2e56","vaultid":"a2d7522d-7afa-4d45-9c0c-6014f37a16ed","uId":"973279be-e77b-4cb6-98e3-7384ee879fac","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"INF","skill_desc":"Inferences","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Henry Ossawa Tanner&rsquo;s 1893 painting <em>The Banjo</em> <em>Lesson</em>, which depicts an elderly man teaching a boy to play the banjo, is regarded as a landmark in the history of works by Black artists in the United States. Scholars should be cautious when ascribing political or ideological values to the painting, however: beliefs and assumptions that are commonly held now may have been unfamiliar to Tanner and his contemporaries, and vice versa. Scholars who forget this fact when discussing <em>The Banjo Lesson</em> therefore <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span></p>","stem":"<p>Which choice most logically completes the text?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"847a310f-f5d3-4b95-a79f-93e41f0f7296","content":"<p>risk judging Tanner&rsquo;s painting by standards that may not be historically appropriate.</p>"},{"id":"8443ee27-0fa4-44c8-911e-311573ded2ab","content":"<p>tend to conflate Tanner&rsquo;s political views with those of his contemporaries.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"a18598d0-7394-419b-ace5-b607ebce9f94","content":"<p>forgo analyzing Tanner&rsquo;s painting in favor of analyzing his political activity.</p>"},{"id":"867ffeb7-997d-4033-86cf-fe773c1a754a","content":"<p>wrongly assume that Tanner&rsquo;s painting was intended as a critique of his fellow artists.&nbsp;</p>"}],"keys":["847a310f-f5d3-4b95-a79f-93e41f0f7296"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer. It most logically completes the text. The text argues that Tanner and his contemporaries may have been unfamiliar with modern beliefs and values. This suggests that scholars who attribute those modern values to Tanner&rsquo;s painting are risking judging the painting by standards that are not historically accurate.&nbsp;&nbsp;</p><p>Choice B is incorrect. It doesn&rsquo;t logically complete the text. The text argues that Tanner AND his contemporaries may have been unfamiliar with modern views. It never suggests that Tanner&rsquo;s views were different from his contemporaries&rsquo; views. &nbsp;Choice C is incorrect. It doesn&rsquo;t logically complete the text. The text never suggests that scholars should analyze Tanner&rsquo;s political activity instead of his painting. Rather, the text argues that Tanner and his contemporaries may have been unfamiliar with modern beliefs and values.&nbsp;Choice D is incorrect. It doesn&rsquo;t logically complete the text. The text never suggests that Tanner wanted to critique his contemporaries with his painting. Rather, the text argues that Tanner AND his contemporaries may have been unfamiliar with modern beliefs and values.&nbsp;</p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-005-INF","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"1cc54c82-ef86-42e7-b69c-58b77ef7b8b1","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959312,"updateDate":1691007959312}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959312, 1691007959312),
    ($SATQ$0e3b4967$SATQ$, $SATQ$41a03d69-174b-43a7-be54-96a52f28ee57$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$254b68d7-5ddc-4013-be0d-8d7af2d60067$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$CID$SATQ$, $SATQ$Central Ideas and Details$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Scrapbooks of saved fabric pieces were commonly kept by women in the nineteenth-century United States, but few are as meticulously detailed as Hannah Ditzler Alspaugh&rsquo;s work. Alongside each piece of fabric, Alspaugh recorded intimate memories, such as dressmaking with her sister. Additionally, she listed the prices and how she used the fabric. Historians note that by representing fifty years of changing textures, patterns, and dress styles, the scrapbook is a record of nineteenth-century textiles and dressmaking as well as Alspaugh&rsquo;s life.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice best states the main idea of the text?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"04cad1be-7df8-4867-8536-c934179db18a","content":"<p>Alspaugh inspired other women to save pieces of fabric in scrapbooks and provide historical records of nineteenth-century fashions in the United States.</p>"},{"id":"c82d55b4-74a6-4161-a7b4-c931f7c68f9b","content":"<p>Historians rely on fabric scrapbooks to understand how fashions changed throughout the nineteenth-century United States.</p>"},{"id":"55e91db7-0079-4ae7-92dc-3784e05e5b3b","content":"<p>Fabric scrapbooks were a popular hobby for many women in the nineteenth-century United States.</p>"},{"id":"54be5049-2fcf-4164-87df-3467cf134176","content":"<p>Alspaugh&rsquo;s scrapbook provides a detailed account of her life and historical record of fashion trends in the nineteenth-century United States.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["54be5049-2fcf-4164-87df-3467cf134176"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is correct. The text describes how Alspaugh&rsquo;s scrapbook is both a record of her life and a historical record of nineteenth-century textiles and dressmaking. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The text says that it was common for American women to keep scrapbooks of fabric pieces in the nineteenth century, and it says that Alspaugh was one of these women. However, it never says that other women were inspired by Alspaugh. Choice B is incorrect. This is too general and too strong. The text says that Alspaugh&rsquo;s scrapbook is a historical record of nineteenth-century textiles and dressmaking, but it never says that historians rely on such scrapbooks in general to understand how fashions changed throughout that time period. This choice also fails to even mention Alspaugh, who is the real focus of the text. Choice C is incorrect. The text does say this, but it&rsquo;s a detail&mdash;not the main idea. The text is mainly about one woman&rsquo;s scrapbook (Alspaugh&rsquo;s), and this choice doesn&rsquo;t even mention her. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-CID-M007$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$5c0093fe-66cd-485f-81a9-b543f1ee1cbb$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"0e3b4967","external_id":"41a03d69-174b-43a7-be54-96a52f28ee57","vaultid":"254b68d7-5ddc-4013-be0d-8d7af2d60067","uId":"e6bc80c6-30c5-4112-af88-e2d2e420e28d","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"CID","skill_desc":"Central Ideas and Details","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Scrapbooks of saved fabric pieces were commonly kept by women in the nineteenth-century United States, but few are as meticulously detailed as Hannah Ditzler Alspaugh&rsquo;s work. Alongside each piece of fabric, Alspaugh recorded intimate memories, such as dressmaking with her sister. Additionally, she listed the prices and how she used the fabric. Historians note that by representing fifty years of changing textures, patterns, and dress styles, the scrapbook is a record of nineteenth-century textiles and dressmaking as well as Alspaugh&rsquo;s life.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice best states the main idea of the text?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"04cad1be-7df8-4867-8536-c934179db18a","content":"<p>Alspaugh inspired other women to save pieces of fabric in scrapbooks and provide historical records of nineteenth-century fashions in the United States.</p>"},{"id":"c82d55b4-74a6-4161-a7b4-c931f7c68f9b","content":"<p>Historians rely on fabric scrapbooks to understand how fashions changed throughout the nineteenth-century United States.</p>"},{"id":"55e91db7-0079-4ae7-92dc-3784e05e5b3b","content":"<p>Fabric scrapbooks were a popular hobby for many women in the nineteenth-century United States.</p>"},{"id":"54be5049-2fcf-4164-87df-3467cf134176","content":"<p>Alspaugh&rsquo;s scrapbook provides a detailed account of her life and historical record of fashion trends in the nineteenth-century United States.</p>"}],"keys":["54be5049-2fcf-4164-87df-3467cf134176"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is correct. The text describes how Alspaugh&rsquo;s scrapbook is both a record of her life and a historical record of nineteenth-century textiles and dressmaking. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The text says that it was common for American women to keep scrapbooks of fabric pieces in the nineteenth century, and it says that Alspaugh was one of these women. However, it never says that other women were inspired by Alspaugh. Choice B is incorrect. This is too general and too strong. The text says that Alspaugh&rsquo;s scrapbook is a historical record of nineteenth-century textiles and dressmaking, but it never says that historians rely on such scrapbooks in general to understand how fashions changed throughout that time period. This choice also fails to even mention Alspaugh, who is the real focus of the text. Choice C is incorrect. The text does say this, but it&rsquo;s a detail&mdash;not the main idea. The text is mainly about one woman&rsquo;s scrapbook (Alspaugh&rsquo;s), and this choice doesn&rsquo;t even mention her. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-CID-M007","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"5c0093fe-66cd-485f-81a9-b543f1ee1cbb","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959360,"updateDate":1691007959360}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959360, 1691007959360),
    ($SATQ$0ec15b5a$SATQ$, $SATQ$23750698-46e4-4295-9df5-522d592417f2$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$COE$SATQ$, $SATQ$Command of Evidence$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p><figure class="table"><table class="gdr"><caption style="caption-side: top;"><p style="text-align: center;">Incorporated and Unincorporated Self-Employment Rates in Four Occupational Fields, 2015</p></caption><thead><tr><th scope="col" style="text-align: center;vertical-align: bottom;">Occupational field</th><th scope="col" style="text-align: center;vertical-align: bottom;">Incorporated self-employment rate</th><th scope="col" style="text-align: center;vertical-align: bottom;">Unincorporated self-employment rate</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row" style="text-align: left;">Construction and resource extraction</th><td style="text-align: center;">4.4%</td><td style="text-align: center;">14.8%</td></tr><tr><th scope="row" style="text-align: left;">Installation, maintenance, and repair</th><td style="text-align: center;">2.7%</td><td style="text-align: center;">6.2%</td></tr><tr><th scope="row" style="text-align: left;">Management, business, and financial services</th><td style="text-align: center;">8.9%</td><td style="text-align: center;">9.7%</td></tr><tr><th scope="row" style="text-align: left;">Sales and related</th><td style="text-align: center;">5.8%</td><td style="text-align: center;">7.8%</td></tr></tbody></table></figure></p>
<p>Self-employed workers&mdash;individuals who are not employees of other individuals or businesses but instead earn their income as business owners, independent contractors, or freelance workers&mdash;make up an important part of the US labor force. In order to receive legal and tax benefits enjoyed by corporations, self-employed individuals may choose to incorporate their business. A 2015 survey of incorporated and unincorporated self-employment rates in four occupational fields showed that the highest incorporated self-employment rate occurred among people working in <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span></p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the text?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"05954fcb-ad3e-4ffb-9477-8186352fdf92","content":"<p>sales and related occupations.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"46e0a877-94a5-4e13-8d6b-ecbdc6c1a333","content":"<p>installation, maintenance, and repair occupations.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"8ba28ea1-ced0-4dbc-9363-bb9baafdac94","content":"<p>construction and resource extraction occupations.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"f0782e47-4a59-4f77-b65b-d5ff71a003e0","content":"<p>management, business, and financial services occupations.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["f0782e47-4a59-4f77-b65b-d5ff71a003e0"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer because it most effectively uses data from the table to complete the text. The text discusses self-employed workers in the US, and the table shows the US incorporated and unincorporated self-employment rates in four occupational fields in 2015. According to the table, the incorporated self-employment rate was 8.9% for the management, business, and financial services occupational field, which is higher than the incorporated self-employment rate shown for any other occupational field.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because according to the table, the incorporated self-employment rate was 5.8% for the sales and related occupational field, which is lower than the 8.9% for the management, business, and financial services occupational field. Choice B is incorrect because according to the table, the incorporated self-employment rate was 2.7% for the installation, maintenance, and repair occupational field, which is lower than the 8.9% for the management, business, and financial services occupational field. Choice C is incorrect because according to the table, the incorporated self-employment rate was 4.4% for the construction and resource extraction occupational field, which is lower than the 8.9% for the management, business, and financial services occupational field.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"0ec15b5a","external_id":"23750698-46e4-4295-9df5-522d592417f2","vaultid":null,"uId":"8f424c65-7763-429f-a30c-551338f871fb","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"COE","skill_desc":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p><figure class=\"table\"><table class=\"gdr\"><caption style=\"caption-side: top;\"><p style=\"text-align: center;\">Incorporated and Unincorporated Self-Employment Rates in Four Occupational Fields, 2015</p></caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\" style=\"text-align: center;vertical-align: bottom;\">Occupational field</th><th scope=\"col\" style=\"text-align: center;vertical-align: bottom;\">Incorporated self-employment rate</th><th scope=\"col\" style=\"text-align: center;vertical-align: bottom;\">Unincorporated self-employment rate</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\" style=\"text-align: left;\">Construction and resource extraction</th><td style=\"text-align: center;\">4.4%</td><td style=\"text-align: center;\">14.8%</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\" style=\"text-align: left;\">Installation, maintenance, and repair</th><td style=\"text-align: center;\">2.7%</td><td style=\"text-align: center;\">6.2%</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\" style=\"text-align: left;\">Management, business, and financial services</th><td style=\"text-align: center;\">8.9%</td><td style=\"text-align: center;\">9.7%</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\" style=\"text-align: left;\">Sales and related</th><td style=\"text-align: center;\">5.8%</td><td style=\"text-align: center;\">7.8%</td></tr></tbody></table></figure></p>\n<p>Self-employed workers&mdash;individuals who are not employees of other individuals or businesses but instead earn their income as business owners, independent contractors, or freelance workers&mdash;make up an important part of the US labor force. In order to receive legal and tax benefits enjoyed by corporations, self-employed individuals may choose to incorporate their business. A 2015 survey of incorporated and unincorporated self-employment rates in four occupational fields showed that the highest incorporated self-employment rate occurred among people working in <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span></p>","stem":"<p>Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the text?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"05954fcb-ad3e-4ffb-9477-8186352fdf92","content":"<p>sales and related occupations.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"46e0a877-94a5-4e13-8d6b-ecbdc6c1a333","content":"<p>installation, maintenance, and repair occupations.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"8ba28ea1-ced0-4dbc-9363-bb9baafdac94","content":"<p>construction and resource extraction occupations.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"f0782e47-4a59-4f77-b65b-d5ff71a003e0","content":"<p>management, business, and financial services occupations.</p>"}],"keys":["f0782e47-4a59-4f77-b65b-d5ff71a003e0"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer because it most effectively uses data from the table to complete the text. The text discusses self-employed workers in the US, and the table shows the US incorporated and unincorporated self-employment rates in four occupational fields in 2015. According to the table, the incorporated self-employment rate was 8.9% for the management, business, and financial services occupational field, which is higher than the incorporated self-employment rate shown for any other occupational field.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because according to the table, the incorporated self-employment rate was 5.8% for the sales and related occupational field, which is lower than the 8.9% for the management, business, and financial services occupational field. Choice B is incorrect because according to the table, the incorporated self-employment rate was 2.7% for the installation, maintenance, and repair occupational field, which is lower than the 8.9% for the management, business, and financial services occupational field. Choice C is incorrect because according to the table, the incorporated self-employment rate was 4.4% for the construction and resource extraction occupational field, which is lower than the 8.9% for the management, business, and financial services occupational field.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323046,"updateDate":1730147323046}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323046, 1730147323046),
    ($SATQ$0fc0a773$SATQ$, $SATQ$17913995-980e-42ce-a499-437d004e0abb$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$COE$SATQ$, $SATQ$Command of Evidence$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The Minneapolis Institute of Art in Minneapolis, Minnesota, has over 90,000 works of art. Digital images of thousands of those works have been put online through the museum&rsquo;s website and the Google Arts &amp; Culture project. One of the images is of <em>Mississippi Delta</em>, a drawing by Siah Armajani. In a paper, a student claims that <span style="text-decoration: underline;" role="region" aria-label="Referenced Content">putting a work from the museum online increases the number of people who experience that work</span>.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which finding, if true, would most directly support the underlined claim?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"7731082a-2c5a-4bf0-9ea2-652a62888e8e","content":"<p>The Minneapolis Institute of Art has several works by Siah Armajani.</p>"},{"id":"d7008b1a-33ba-4c7c-a214-af653a875ab0","content":"<p>Many people who have been to Minneapolis say that the Minneapolis Institute of Art is worth visiting.</p>"},{"id":"99060996-4964-4e85-b7a2-46c05d5dd5e9","content":"<p><em>Mississippi Delta</em> has been praised by some art critics.</p>"},{"id":"3d468fe8-6f79-4c29-96be-674a3888906c","content":"<p>Each year, more people access the online image of <em>Mississippi Delta</em> than visit the Minneapolis Institute of Art in person.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["3d468fe8-6f79-4c29-96be-674a3888906c"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer because it presents a finding that, if true, would most directly support the student&rsquo;s claim that putting a work online increases the number of people who experience it. The text states that the Minneapolis Institute of Art has more than 90,000 artworks. It goes on to explain that images of a subset of these works can be viewed online, such as an image of Armajani&rsquo;s drawing <em>Mississippi Delta</em>. The text then presents a student&rsquo;s claim that online images of art can increase the number of people who experience the work. If more people view Armajani&rsquo;s drawing online than they do in person, this finding would support the student&rsquo;s claim about the benefits of putting a work from the museum online. Thus, this finding would most directly support the claim because, if it were true, more people would view <em>Mississippi Delta</em> online than would visit the entire museum in person.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because information about the museum having several works by Armajani wouldn&rsquo;t address the student&rsquo;s claim about how putting artworks online affects the number of people who experience them. The quantity of works by one artist that are held in a museum collection wouldn&rsquo;t support a claim about audience size or the impact of online accessibility. Choice B is incorrect because opinions about the museum would only relate to the institution&rsquo;s reputation. This finding wouldn&rsquo;t provide information about online accessibility or whether digital images increase the number of people who view specific artworks. Choice C is incorrect because critics&rsquo; praise for <em>Mississippi Delta</em> wouldn&rsquo;t address the student&rsquo;s claim about how online access increases the number of people who can experience a work. Although positive comments from art critics could affect interest in an artwork, this finding wouldn&rsquo;t provide information about how many people experience the work or whether online accessibility increases that number.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"0fc0a773","external_id":"17913995-980e-42ce-a499-437d004e0abb","vaultid":null,"uId":"37fb0024-4f62-41b1-bcd1-bf7b4eb42505","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"COE","skill_desc":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":2,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The Minneapolis Institute of Art in Minneapolis, Minnesota, has over 90,000 works of art. Digital images of thousands of those works have been put online through the museum&rsquo;s website and the Google Arts &amp; Culture project. One of the images is of <em>Mississippi Delta</em>, a drawing by Siah Armajani. In a paper, a student claims that <span style=\"text-decoration: underline;\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Referenced Content\">putting a work from the museum online increases the number of people who experience that work</span>.</p>","stem":"<p>Which finding, if true, would most directly support the underlined claim?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"7731082a-2c5a-4bf0-9ea2-652a62888e8e","content":"<p>The Minneapolis Institute of Art has several works by Siah Armajani.</p>"},{"id":"d7008b1a-33ba-4c7c-a214-af653a875ab0","content":"<p>Many people who have been to Minneapolis say that the Minneapolis Institute of Art is worth visiting.</p>"},{"id":"99060996-4964-4e85-b7a2-46c05d5dd5e9","content":"<p><em>Mississippi Delta</em> has been praised by some art critics.</p>"},{"id":"3d468fe8-6f79-4c29-96be-674a3888906c","content":"<p>Each year, more people access the online image of <em>Mississippi Delta</em> than visit the Minneapolis Institute of Art in person.</p>"}],"keys":["3d468fe8-6f79-4c29-96be-674a3888906c"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer because it presents a finding that, if true, would most directly support the student&rsquo;s claim that putting a work online increases the number of people who experience it. The text states that the Minneapolis Institute of Art has more than 90,000 artworks. It goes on to explain that images of a subset of these works can be viewed online, such as an image of Armajani&rsquo;s drawing <em>Mississippi Delta</em>. The text then presents a student&rsquo;s claim that online images of art can increase the number of people who experience the work. If more people view Armajani&rsquo;s drawing online than they do in person, this finding would support the student&rsquo;s claim about the benefits of putting a work from the museum online. Thus, this finding would most directly support the claim because, if it were true, more people would view <em>Mississippi Delta</em> online than would visit the entire museum in person.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because information about the museum having several works by Armajani wouldn&rsquo;t address the student&rsquo;s claim about how putting artworks online affects the number of people who experience them. The quantity of works by one artist that are held in a museum collection wouldn&rsquo;t support a claim about audience size or the impact of online accessibility. Choice B is incorrect because opinions about the museum would only relate to the institution&rsquo;s reputation. This finding wouldn&rsquo;t provide information about online accessibility or whether digital images increase the number of people who view specific artworks. Choice C is incorrect because critics&rsquo; praise for <em>Mississippi Delta</em> wouldn&rsquo;t address the student&rsquo;s claim about how online access increases the number of people who can experience a work. Although positive comments from art critics could affect interest in an artwork, this finding wouldn&rsquo;t provide information about how many people experience the work or whether online accessibility increases that number.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1755117061175,"updateDate":1755117061175}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1755117061175, 1755117061175),
    ($SATQ$0fd96039$SATQ$, $SATQ$92264ffd-d9d5-4e5f-b423-5c7112ae7b48$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$CID$SATQ$, $SATQ$Central Ideas and Details$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>It has long been thought that the original author of the US Pledge of Allegiance was Francis Bellamy, who said that he wrote the pledge one evening in August of <span data-ssml-say-as="date">1892</span>. But a historian recently discovered a newspaper article that was published several months before August <span data-ssml-say-as="date">1892</span>. The article describes students reciting the same pledge that Bellamy claims he first wrote in August. This means that Bellamy may not have created the pledge after all.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Based on the text, what piece of knowledge about the Pledge of Allegiance has recently become uncertain?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"38d3c168-6be3-4a58-b7a4-03dffcb7ef86","content":"<p>How many changes were made to the pledge in the 1900s</p>"},{"id":"98666e9f-79a2-44b1-a761-842bb732a913","content":"<p>The identity of the person who first wrote the pledge</p>"},{"id":"63e5612e-2856-4681-98d4-d7eae525fd19","content":"<p>The name of the newspaper that first published the pledge</p>"},{"id":"14e41818-f646-4d51-82c7-f518be2b5561","content":"<p>Which part of the pledge students liked best in <span data-ssml-say-as=\"date\">1892</span></p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["98666e9f-79a2-44b1-a761-842bb732a913"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer because it presents a supposed fact about the Pledge of Allegiance that, as the text explains, has recently become uncertain. The text begins by explaining the long-held belief that the original author of the US Pledge of Allegiance was Francis Bellamy, who claimed to have written it in August 1892. The text then describes a historian&rsquo;s recent discovery of a newspaper article published several months before August 1892 that mentions students reciting the same pledge. The text concludes that this discovery suggests Bellamy may not have created the pledge after all. Thus, what has recently become uncertain is the identity of the person who first wrote the pledge.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t address any changes made to the Pledge of Allegiance in the 1900s. The text focuses solely on the question of who originally authored the pledge in or before 1892 and doesn&rsquo;t mention any subsequent modifications to its wording. Choice C is incorrect. While the text does mention a historian&rsquo;s discovery of a newspaper article that described students reciting the pledge, it doesn&rsquo;t suggest that the pledge was published in its entirety within that article. Moreover, the text doesn&rsquo;t identify the newspaper by name or suggest the possibility of an earlier mention of the pledge in a different newspaper. Choice D is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t mention which parts of the Pledge of Allegiance students preferred in 1892 or at any other time. The text only states that a newspaper article described students reciting the pledge, without providing any information about their preferences regarding different portions of it.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"0fd96039","external_id":"92264ffd-d9d5-4e5f-b423-5c7112ae7b48","vaultid":null,"uId":"9d50a1b8-c911-4bd4-a072-a8f9debea3f6","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"CID","skill_desc":"Central Ideas and Details","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":2,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>It has long been thought that the original author of the US Pledge of Allegiance was Francis Bellamy, who said that he wrote the pledge one evening in August of <span data-ssml-say-as=\"date\">1892</span>. But a historian recently discovered a newspaper article that was published several months before August <span data-ssml-say-as=\"date\">1892</span>. The article describes students reciting the same pledge that Bellamy claims he first wrote in August. This means that Bellamy may not have created the pledge after all.</p>","stem":"<p>Based on the text, what piece of knowledge about the Pledge of Allegiance has recently become uncertain?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"38d3c168-6be3-4a58-b7a4-03dffcb7ef86","content":"<p>How many changes were made to the pledge in the 1900s</p>"},{"id":"98666e9f-79a2-44b1-a761-842bb732a913","content":"<p>The identity of the person who first wrote the pledge</p>"},{"id":"63e5612e-2856-4681-98d4-d7eae525fd19","content":"<p>The name of the newspaper that first published the pledge</p>"},{"id":"14e41818-f646-4d51-82c7-f518be2b5561","content":"<p>Which part of the pledge students liked best in <span data-ssml-say-as=\"date\">1892</span></p>"}],"keys":["98666e9f-79a2-44b1-a761-842bb732a913"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer because it presents a supposed fact about the Pledge of Allegiance that, as the text explains, has recently become uncertain. The text begins by explaining the long-held belief that the original author of the US Pledge of Allegiance was Francis Bellamy, who claimed to have written it in August 1892. The text then describes a historian&rsquo;s recent discovery of a newspaper article published several months before August 1892 that mentions students reciting the same pledge. The text concludes that this discovery suggests Bellamy may not have created the pledge after all. Thus, what has recently become uncertain is the identity of the person who first wrote the pledge.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t address any changes made to the Pledge of Allegiance in the 1900s. The text focuses solely on the question of who originally authored the pledge in or before 1892 and doesn&rsquo;t mention any subsequent modifications to its wording. Choice C is incorrect. While the text does mention a historian&rsquo;s discovery of a newspaper article that described students reciting the pledge, it doesn&rsquo;t suggest that the pledge was published in its entirety within that article. Moreover, the text doesn&rsquo;t identify the newspaper by name or suggest the possibility of an earlier mention of the pledge in a different newspaper. Choice D is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t mention which parts of the Pledge of Allegiance students preferred in 1892 or at any other time. The text only states that a newspaper article described students reciting the pledge, without providing any information about their preferences regarding different portions of it.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1776362771169,"updateDate":1776362771169}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1776362771169, 1776362771169),
    ($SATQ$11a9f635$SATQ$, $SATQ$938fef9c-6c98-458a-8ce2-236a09a80f22$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$8d2503df-279c-4df1-95f8-6ddb32870367$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$CID$SATQ$, $SATQ$Central Ideas and Details$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Paleontologists searching for signs of ancient life have found many fossilized specimens of prehistoric human ancestors, including several from the Pleistocene era discovered in a geological formation in the Minatogawa quarry in Japan. However, to study the emergence of the earliest multicellular organisms to appear on Earth, researchers must turn elsewhere, such as to the Ediacaran geological formation at Mistaken Point in Canada. A UNESCO World Heritage Site, the 146-hectare reserve contains more than 10,000 fossils that together document a critical moment in evolutionary history.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>What does the text indicate about the geological formation at Mistaken Point?&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"9c3ded22-c5a3-4417-8caf-905eab29acc1","content":"<p>It holds a greater number of fossils but from a smaller variety of species than the formation in the Minatogawa quarry does.</p>"},{"id":"9ae8e97f-cb04-47f1-b17a-4d4517a49d85","content":"<p>It has provided evidence that the earliest human species may have emerged before the Pleistocene era.</p>"},{"id":"9085e554-aa74-4fe4-9a55-eeaad568c7fa","content":"<p>It is widely considered by paleontologists to be the most valuable source of information about prehistoric life forms.</p>"},{"id":"f5c88851-1073-4f04-a9f2-a05d0dd75439","content":"<p>It contains specimens from an older time period than those found in the formation in the Minatogawa quarry.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["f5c88851-1073-4f04-a9f2-a05d0dd75439"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. The text says that the formation at Mistaken Point contains fossils of &ldquo;the earliest multicellular organisms,&rdquo; which implies that these fossils are from an older time period than the fossils of &ldquo;prehistoric human ancestors&rdquo; found in the Minatogawa quarry. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The text says that the formation at Mistake Point contains &ldquo;more than 10,000 fossils,&rdquo; but it doesn&rsquo;t compare this number to the number of fossils in the Minatogawa quarry. It also doesn&rsquo;t say anything about the variety of species in either formation. Choice B is incorrect. The text says that the formation at Mistaken Point contains fossils of &ldquo;the earliest multicellular organisms,&rdquo; but it never says that the site contains early human fossils too. Rather, the early human fossils mentioned in the text were found in the formation at Minatogawa quarry. Choice C is incorrect. The text says that the fossils at Mistaken Point &ldquo;document a critical moment in evolutionary history,&rdquo; but it never says that Mistaken Point is the most valuable source of information about prehistoric life forms. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-CID-M010$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$2917455e-2cc0-4aff-aae0-0b12e910f4cf$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"11a9f635","external_id":"938fef9c-6c98-458a-8ce2-236a09a80f22","vaultid":"8d2503df-279c-4df1-95f8-6ddb32870367","uId":"10240f21-abe3-4561-91e8-5fb8716a3aef","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"CID","skill_desc":"Central Ideas and Details","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Paleontologists searching for signs of ancient life have found many fossilized specimens of prehistoric human ancestors, including several from the Pleistocene era discovered in a geological formation in the Minatogawa quarry in Japan. However, to study the emergence of the earliest multicellular organisms to appear on Earth, researchers must turn elsewhere, such as to the Ediacaran geological formation at Mistaken Point in Canada. A UNESCO World Heritage Site, the 146-hectare reserve contains more than 10,000 fossils that together document a critical moment in evolutionary history.</p>","stem":"<p>What does the text indicate about the geological formation at Mistaken Point?&nbsp;</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"9c3ded22-c5a3-4417-8caf-905eab29acc1","content":"<p>It holds a greater number of fossils but from a smaller variety of species than the formation in the Minatogawa quarry does.</p>"},{"id":"9ae8e97f-cb04-47f1-b17a-4d4517a49d85","content":"<p>It has provided evidence that the earliest human species may have emerged before the Pleistocene era.</p>"},{"id":"9085e554-aa74-4fe4-9a55-eeaad568c7fa","content":"<p>It is widely considered by paleontologists to be the most valuable source of information about prehistoric life forms.</p>"},{"id":"f5c88851-1073-4f04-a9f2-a05d0dd75439","content":"<p>It contains specimens from an older time period than those found in the formation in the Minatogawa quarry.</p>"}],"keys":["f5c88851-1073-4f04-a9f2-a05d0dd75439"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer. The text says that the formation at Mistaken Point contains fossils of &ldquo;the earliest multicellular organisms,&rdquo; which implies that these fossils are from an older time period than the fossils of &ldquo;prehistoric human ancestors&rdquo; found in the Minatogawa quarry. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The text says that the formation at Mistake Point contains &ldquo;more than 10,000 fossils,&rdquo; but it doesn&rsquo;t compare this number to the number of fossils in the Minatogawa quarry. It also doesn&rsquo;t say anything about the variety of species in either formation. Choice B is incorrect. The text says that the formation at Mistaken Point contains fossils of &ldquo;the earliest multicellular organisms,&rdquo; but it never says that the site contains early human fossils too. Rather, the early human fossils mentioned in the text were found in the formation at Minatogawa quarry. Choice C is incorrect. The text says that the fossils at Mistaken Point &ldquo;document a critical moment in evolutionary history,&rdquo; but it never says that Mistaken Point is the most valuable source of information about prehistoric life forms. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-CID-M010","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"2917455e-2cc0-4aff-aae0-0b12e910f4cf","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959360,"updateDate":1691007959360}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959360, 1691007959360),
    ($SATQ$11c00ab9$SATQ$, $SATQ$cbd580d7-1b7a-452d-8eb4-06f8db889ec7$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$COE$SATQ$, $SATQ$Command of Evidence$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p><figure class="table"><table class="gdr"><caption style="caption-side: top;"><p style="text-align: center;">Estimated Impacts of Invasive Predator Species on Threatened Birds, Mammals, and Reptiles</p></caption><thead><tr><th scope="col" style="text-align: center;vertical-align: bottom;">Invasive predator species</th><th scope="col" style="text-align: center;vertical-align: bottom;">Estimated impact on threatened birds</th><th scope="col" style="text-align: center;vertical-align: bottom;">Estimated impact on threatened mammals</th><th scope="col" style="text-align: center;vertical-align: bottom;">Estimated impact on threatened reptiles</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row" style="text-align: left;">Mongooses</th><td style="text-align: center;">0.66</td><td style="text-align: center;">0.75</td><td style="text-align: center;">0.44</td></tr><tr><th scope="row" style="text-align: left;">Dogs</th><td style="text-align: center;">0.67</td><td style="text-align: center;">0.57</td><td style="text-align: center;">0.44</td></tr><tr><th scope="row" style="text-align: left;">Cats</th><td style="text-align: center;">0.74</td><td style="text-align: center;">0.61</td><td style="text-align: center;">0.52</td></tr></tbody></table></figure></p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>How harmful are invasive predators to threatened species? To find out, researchers assigned values from 0.25 to 1.00 to various harms caused by invasive predators. The researchers then used these values to estimate the extent to which several invasive predator species have harmfully impacted threatened birds, mammals, and reptiles. In the table, higher numbers represent greater impacts. The estimated impact of invasive mongooses on threatened mammals is <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span></p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the example?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"0f30f9ab-c677-439a-892e-beca9def9350","content":"<p>0.35.</p>"},{"id":"c16a1456-2bb7-4ef4-b5a0-604db573339d","content":"<p>0.61.</p>"},{"id":"bc22b3d6-0eb5-40a2-9f82-eb5bcbd588d2","content":"<p>0.75.</p>"},{"id":"165e6c3e-6c98-42b6-9f74-4f9fda6eeff2","content":"<p>0.52.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["bc22b3d6-0eb5-40a2-9f82-eb5bcbd588d2"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer because it effectively uses data from the table to complete the example of the estimated impact of invasive mongooses on threatened mammals. The table shows the values for the estimated harmful impact of three invasive predator species (mongooses, dogs, and cats) on threatened birds, mammals, and reptiles. The text states that the values are on a scale from 0.25 (less impact) to 1.00 (greater impact). The table shows that the value for the estimated impact of invasive mongooses on threatened mammals is 0.75.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the table shows that the value for the estimated impact of mongooses on threatened mammals is 0.75, not 0.35. The value 0.35 is not assigned to any of the categories in the table. Choice B is incorrect because the table shows that 0.61 is the value for the estimated impact of cats, not mongooses, on threatened mammals. Choice D is incorrect because the table shows that 0.52 is the value for the estimated impact of cats on threatened reptiles, not of mongooses on threatened mammals.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"11c00ab9","external_id":"cbd580d7-1b7a-452d-8eb4-06f8db889ec7","vaultid":null,"uId":"eb057890-81ae-4b4b-b0df-4863a6a654b5","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"COE","skill_desc":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p><figure class=\"table\"><table class=\"gdr\"><caption style=\"caption-side: top;\"><p style=\"text-align: center;\">Estimated Impacts of Invasive Predator Species on Threatened Birds, Mammals, and Reptiles</p></caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\" style=\"text-align: center;vertical-align: bottom;\">Invasive predator species</th><th scope=\"col\" style=\"text-align: center;vertical-align: bottom;\">Estimated impact on threatened birds</th><th scope=\"col\" style=\"text-align: center;vertical-align: bottom;\">Estimated impact on threatened mammals</th><th scope=\"col\" style=\"text-align: center;vertical-align: bottom;\">Estimated impact on threatened reptiles</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\" style=\"text-align: left;\">Mongooses</th><td style=\"text-align: center;\">0.66</td><td style=\"text-align: center;\">0.75</td><td style=\"text-align: center;\">0.44</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\" style=\"text-align: left;\">Dogs</th><td style=\"text-align: center;\">0.67</td><td style=\"text-align: center;\">0.57</td><td style=\"text-align: center;\">0.44</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\" style=\"text-align: left;\">Cats</th><td style=\"text-align: center;\">0.74</td><td style=\"text-align: center;\">0.61</td><td style=\"text-align: center;\">0.52</td></tr></tbody></table></figure></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p>How harmful are invasive predators to threatened species? To find out, researchers assigned values from 0.25 to 1.00 to various harms caused by invasive predators. The researchers then used these values to estimate the extent to which several invasive predator species have harmfully impacted threatened birds, mammals, and reptiles. In the table, higher numbers represent greater impacts. The estimated impact of invasive mongooses on threatened mammals is <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span></p>","stem":"<p>Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the example?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"0f30f9ab-c677-439a-892e-beca9def9350","content":"<p>0.35.</p>"},{"id":"c16a1456-2bb7-4ef4-b5a0-604db573339d","content":"<p>0.61.</p>"},{"id":"bc22b3d6-0eb5-40a2-9f82-eb5bcbd588d2","content":"<p>0.75.</p>"},{"id":"165e6c3e-6c98-42b6-9f74-4f9fda6eeff2","content":"<p>0.52.</p>"}],"keys":["bc22b3d6-0eb5-40a2-9f82-eb5bcbd588d2"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer because it effectively uses data from the table to complete the example of the estimated impact of invasive mongooses on threatened mammals. The table shows the values for the estimated harmful impact of three invasive predator species (mongooses, dogs, and cats) on threatened birds, mammals, and reptiles. The text states that the values are on a scale from 0.25 (less impact) to 1.00 (greater impact). The table shows that the value for the estimated impact of invasive mongooses on threatened mammals is 0.75.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the table shows that the value for the estimated impact of mongooses on threatened mammals is 0.75, not 0.35. The value 0.35 is not assigned to any of the categories in the table. Choice B is incorrect because the table shows that 0.61 is the value for the estimated impact of cats, not mongooses, on threatened mammals. Choice D is incorrect because the table shows that 0.52 is the value for the estimated impact of cats on threatened reptiles, not of mongooses on threatened mammals.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323046,"updateDate":1730147323046}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323046, 1730147323046),
    ($SATQ$11c68ded$SATQ$, $SATQ$30c2d8b8-5292-4af7-b701-0109496da1a5$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$CID$SATQ$, $SATQ$Central Ideas and Details$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>A contraction of &ldquo;you all,&rdquo; the pronoun &ldquo;y&rsquo;all&rdquo; has long been used as a plural version of &ldquo;you&rdquo; in the South and in Black communities around the US. In recent decades, most other English-speaking communities in the US have begun to use &ldquo;y&rsquo;all.&rdquo; What explains its rise in popularity? Many varieties of English have no pronoun that specifically addresses more than one person and instead must use &ldquo;you&rdquo; to address both one person and more than one. But &ldquo;y&rsquo;all&rdquo; always refers to two or more people. As a result, it conveys the speaker&rsquo;s meaning more precisely than &ldquo;you&rdquo; can.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which question does the text most directly attempt to answer?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"fc435544-661f-4c03-ba75-8001a03b302d","content":"<p>How many other plural versions of the pronoun &ldquo;you&rdquo; are there in English, besides &ldquo;y&rsquo;all&rdquo;?</p>"},{"id":"e97e64ec-6124-41c1-aeb6-76edaf84932f","content":"<p>Why has the pronoun &ldquo;y&rsquo;all&rdquo; become more widely used in the US?</p>"},{"id":"e5f7748e-69dd-4aa6-bcd7-09a1c2029afc","content":"<p>When was the first recorded use of the pronoun &ldquo;y&rsquo;all&rdquo; in the English language?</p>"},{"id":"cf5386b8-d32f-4aba-86c2-d143d8f624b3","content":"<p>Is &ldquo;y&rsquo;all&rdquo; commonly used in English-speaking regions of the world besides the US?</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["e97e64ec-6124-41c1-aeb6-76edaf84932f"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer because it presents a question that the text is attempting to answer: why has the pronoun "y&rsquo;all" become more widely used in the US? The text begins by explaining where and how the plural pronoun "y&rsquo;all" originated and then goes on to state that its use has been rising in popularity, even in areas outside of its place of origin. The text then attributes this rise in popularity to the fact that many varieties of English do not have a pronoun to address more than one person, and thus "you" must function as both a singular and plural pronoun.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because while the text states that "y&rsquo;all" is used as a plural of "you" in English, it does not discuss other plural forms of the word. Choice C is incorrect because while the text discusses the general origins of the pronoun "y&rsquo;all," it does not state when the use of the pronoun was first recorded in the English language. Choice D is incorrect because though the text addresses the use of the pronoun "y&rsquo;all" within English-speaking communities in the US, it does not address its use outside of that geographic area.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"11c68ded","external_id":"30c2d8b8-5292-4af7-b701-0109496da1a5","vaultid":null,"uId":"12bddabf-0cc4-4fc4-866c-898df440370f","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"CID","skill_desc":"Central Ideas and Details","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>A contraction of &ldquo;you all,&rdquo; the pronoun &ldquo;y&rsquo;all&rdquo; has long been used as a plural version of &ldquo;you&rdquo; in the South and in Black communities around the US. In recent decades, most other English-speaking communities in the US have begun to use &ldquo;y&rsquo;all.&rdquo; What explains its rise in popularity? Many varieties of English have no pronoun that specifically addresses more than one person and instead must use &ldquo;you&rdquo; to address both one person and more than one. But &ldquo;y&rsquo;all&rdquo; always refers to two or more people. As a result, it conveys the speaker&rsquo;s meaning more precisely than &ldquo;you&rdquo; can.</p>","stem":"<p>Which question does the text most directly attempt to answer?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"fc435544-661f-4c03-ba75-8001a03b302d","content":"<p>How many other plural versions of the pronoun &ldquo;you&rdquo; are there in English, besides &ldquo;y&rsquo;all&rdquo;?</p>"},{"id":"e97e64ec-6124-41c1-aeb6-76edaf84932f","content":"<p>Why has the pronoun &ldquo;y&rsquo;all&rdquo; become more widely used in the US?</p>"},{"id":"e5f7748e-69dd-4aa6-bcd7-09a1c2029afc","content":"<p>When was the first recorded use of the pronoun &ldquo;y&rsquo;all&rdquo; in the English language?</p>"},{"id":"cf5386b8-d32f-4aba-86c2-d143d8f624b3","content":"<p>Is &ldquo;y&rsquo;all&rdquo; commonly used in English-speaking regions of the world besides the US?</p>"}],"keys":["e97e64ec-6124-41c1-aeb6-76edaf84932f"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer because it presents a question that the text is attempting to answer: why has the pronoun \"y&rsquo;all\" become more widely used in the US? The text begins by explaining where and how the plural pronoun \"y&rsquo;all\" originated and then goes on to state that its use has been rising in popularity, even in areas outside of its place of origin. The text then attributes this rise in popularity to the fact that many varieties of English do not have a pronoun to address more than one person, and thus \"you\" must function as both a singular and plural pronoun.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because while the text states that \"y&rsquo;all\" is used as a plural of \"you\" in English, it does not discuss other plural forms of the word. Choice C is incorrect because while the text discusses the general origins of the pronoun \"y&rsquo;all,\" it does not state when the use of the pronoun was first recorded in the English language. Choice D is incorrect because though the text addresses the use of the pronoun \"y&rsquo;all\" within English-speaking communities in the US, it does not address its use outside of that geographic area.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323040,"updateDate":1730147323040}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323040, 1730147323040),
    ($SATQ$12030076$SATQ$, $SATQ$70a3b3f6-b02a-4d56-93e8-aec50bc76eca$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$fd4675b3-6c3f-43c3-9016-a4dedb5fa3d0$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$CID$SATQ$, $SATQ$Central Ideas and Details$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>NASA&rsquo;s Aspera mission, led by Carlos Vargas, will investigate the circumgalactic medium (CGM), the huge swaths of low-density gas that fill and surround galaxies. Specifically, the team will focus on portions of the gas that exist in a &ldquo;warm-hot&rdquo; phase: these portions haven&rsquo;t previously been observable but are thought to fuel new star formation and hold most of the mass that makes up a galaxy. Using a telescope capable of revealing these parts of the CGM, the Aspera mission should help answer long-standing questions about how galaxies emerge, change, and even interact.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice best states the main idea of the text?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"c292bff8-d758-41dc-b95f-714e4fa3deb5","content":"<p>As the leader of NASA&rsquo;s Aspera mission, Vargas will be the first person to investigate the makeup of the CGM.</p>"},{"id":"80ed03ec-bec2-4d25-bb8f-4db5dfce2d96","content":"<p>Although galaxies that are surrounded by the CGM have been studied, researchers have been unable to directly observe low-density gas in the CGM in the &ldquo;warm-hot&rdquo; phase.</p>"},{"id":"2819abef-530c-43f8-944a-eaeb6fbc5fc6","content":"<p>Researchers don&rsquo;t yet have a complete understanding of the process of galaxy evolution but have raised the possibility that galaxies interact with each other at times.</p>"},{"id":"6f4b881c-655c-47cb-b13e-016bb367947b","content":"<p>The Aspera mission is expected to produce the first direct observations of CGM gas in the &ldquo;warm-hot&rdquo; phase, which likely has an important role in the evolution of galaxies.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["6f4b881c-655c-47cb-b13e-016bb367947b"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer because it most accurately states the main idea of the text. The text begins by mentioning NASA&rsquo;s Aspera mission, which will investigate the low-density gas that makes up the circumgalactic medium (CGM). According to the text, this mission will focus on a portion of the CGM&rsquo;s gas that exists in a &ldquo;warm-hot&rdquo; phase; this &ldquo;warm-hot&rdquo; gas has not been previously observed, but it is thought to make up most of the mass of galaxies and play a part in star formation. Finally, the text mentions a telescope capable of examining this previously unobservable &ldquo;warm-hot&rdquo; gas: the Aspera mission will use this telescope in the hope of answering questions about galaxy formation and change. Therefore, the main idea of the text is that the Aspera mission is likely to produce the first direct observations of CGM gas in the &ldquo;warm-hot&rdquo; phase, which likely has an important role in the evolution of galaxies. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. Although this choice mentions the Aspera mission, names its leader, and generally states the mission&rsquo;s purpose, it does not reference the &ldquo;warm-hot&rdquo; gas or fully convey the reason why the Aspera mission is significant. Choice B is incorrect. Although this choice mentions the &ldquo;warm-hot&rdquo; gas that makes up a portion of the CGM, it does not reference the Aspera mission or describe its importance. The text also does not mention that galaxies surrounded by the CGM have been studied. Choice C is incorrect. Although this choice describes a problem related to the CGM that researchers have been attempting to solve and presents the speculation of those researchers, it does not mention the Aspera mission or describe its purpose. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-037-CID$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$9c11766f-cefe-4eae-b94a-777f06b09562$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"12030076","external_id":"70a3b3f6-b02a-4d56-93e8-aec50bc76eca","vaultid":"fd4675b3-6c3f-43c3-9016-a4dedb5fa3d0","uId":"7f32d9dd-647e-4562-9d34-b16b097f55ea","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"CID","skill_desc":"Central Ideas and Details","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>NASA&rsquo;s Aspera mission, led by Carlos Vargas, will investigate the circumgalactic medium (CGM), the huge swaths of low-density gas that fill and surround galaxies. Specifically, the team will focus on portions of the gas that exist in a &ldquo;warm-hot&rdquo; phase: these portions haven&rsquo;t previously been observable but are thought to fuel new star formation and hold most of the mass that makes up a galaxy. Using a telescope capable of revealing these parts of the CGM, the Aspera mission should help answer long-standing questions about how galaxies emerge, change, and even interact.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice best states the main idea of the text?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"c292bff8-d758-41dc-b95f-714e4fa3deb5","content":"<p>As the leader of NASA&rsquo;s Aspera mission, Vargas will be the first person to investigate the makeup of the CGM.</p>"},{"id":"80ed03ec-bec2-4d25-bb8f-4db5dfce2d96","content":"<p>Although galaxies that are surrounded by the CGM have been studied, researchers have been unable to directly observe low-density gas in the CGM in the &ldquo;warm-hot&rdquo; phase.</p>"},{"id":"2819abef-530c-43f8-944a-eaeb6fbc5fc6","content":"<p>Researchers don&rsquo;t yet have a complete understanding of the process of galaxy evolution but have raised the possibility that galaxies interact with each other at times.</p>"},{"id":"6f4b881c-655c-47cb-b13e-016bb367947b","content":"<p>The Aspera mission is expected to produce the first direct observations of CGM gas in the &ldquo;warm-hot&rdquo; phase, which likely has an important role in the evolution of galaxies.</p>"}],"keys":["6f4b881c-655c-47cb-b13e-016bb367947b"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer because it most accurately states the main idea of the text. The text begins by mentioning NASA&rsquo;s Aspera mission, which will investigate the low-density gas that makes up the circumgalactic medium (CGM). According to the text, this mission will focus on a portion of the CGM&rsquo;s gas that exists in a &ldquo;warm-hot&rdquo; phase; this &ldquo;warm-hot&rdquo; gas has not been previously observed, but it is thought to make up most of the mass of galaxies and play a part in star formation. Finally, the text mentions a telescope capable of examining this previously unobservable &ldquo;warm-hot&rdquo; gas: the Aspera mission will use this telescope in the hope of answering questions about galaxy formation and change. Therefore, the main idea of the text is that the Aspera mission is likely to produce the first direct observations of CGM gas in the &ldquo;warm-hot&rdquo; phase, which likely has an important role in the evolution of galaxies. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. Although this choice mentions the Aspera mission, names its leader, and generally states the mission&rsquo;s purpose, it does not reference the &ldquo;warm-hot&rdquo; gas or fully convey the reason why the Aspera mission is significant. Choice B is incorrect. Although this choice mentions the &ldquo;warm-hot&rdquo; gas that makes up a portion of the CGM, it does not reference the Aspera mission or describe its importance. The text also does not mention that galaxies surrounded by the CGM have been studied. Choice C is incorrect. Although this choice describes a problem related to the CGM that researchers have been attempting to solve and presents the speculation of those researchers, it does not mention the Aspera mission or describe its purpose. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-037-CID","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"9c11766f-cefe-4eae-b94a-777f06b09562","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959320,"updateDate":1691007959320}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959320, 1691007959320),
    ($SATQ$123bd312$SATQ$, $SATQ$d611db07-c76b-4fcd-97f2-12bb13024b07$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$dd8a1c0e-f827-42e5-9553-cd89efb18b68$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$INF$SATQ$, $SATQ$Inferences$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Herbivorous sauropod dinosaurs could grow more than 100 feet long and weigh up to 80 tons, and some researchers have attributed the evolution of sauropods to such massive sizes to increased plant production resulting from high levels of atmospheric carbon dioxide during the Mesozoic era. However, there is no evidence of significant spikes in carbon dioxide levels coinciding with relevant periods in sauropod evolution, such as when the first large sauropods appeared, when several sauropod lineages underwent further evolution toward gigantism, or when sauropods reached their maximum known sizes, suggesting that <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span></p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice most logically completes the text?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"c1ea4ee7-0cd4-4501-a3e2-1754ea4b7a74","content":"<p>fluctuations in atmospheric carbon dioxide affected different sauropod lineages differently.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"682dd2cc-756e-4c1e-ab6b-7a8ffd00a810","content":"<p>the evolution of larger body sizes in sauropods did not depend on increased atmospheric carbon dioxide.</p>"},{"id":"70b25aec-6644-4c28-bfbd-b7b817ad24fe","content":"<p>atmospheric carbon dioxide was higher when the largest known sauropods lived than it was when the first sauropods appeared.</p>"},{"id":"d17fc41d-2023-483f-88a0-ad7f35e7eb86","content":"<p>sauropods probably would not have evolved to such immense sizes if atmospheric carbon dioxide had been even slightly higher.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["682dd2cc-756e-4c1e-ab6b-7a8ffd00a810"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer because it presents the conclusion that most logically follows from the text&rsquo;s discussion of the relationship between atmospheric carbon dioxide and sauropod body size. The text establishes that sauropods evolved to reach enormous sizes, and it notes that some scientists have asserted that the cause of this phenomenon was increased plant production that resulted from increased atmospheric carbon dioxide. The text goes on to state, however, that atmospheric carbon dioxide levels didn&rsquo;t increase around the time of important periods in sauropods&rsquo; evolution of larger body sizes. If significant periods of sauropod evolution toward larger sizes occurred without increased atmospheric carbon dioxide levels, that suggests that the evolution of larger sizes didn&rsquo;t depend on increased carbon dioxide in the atmosphere.&nbsp;</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t describe any fluctuations in atmospheric carbon dioxide, so there&rsquo;s no evidence in the text to support the conclusion that such fluctuations had different effects on different sauropod lineages. All that the text says about atmospheric carbon dioxide levels is that there weren&rsquo;t increases at particular points that correspond with key moments in sauropod evolution.&nbsp;Choice C is incorrect because the text indicates that there weren&rsquo;t significant increases in atmospheric carbon dioxide around the time of important periods in sauropods&rsquo; evolution toward larger body sizes, not that atmospheric carbon dioxide was higher when the largest sauropods lived than when sauropods first appeared.&nbsp;Choice D is incorrect because the text indicates that atmospheric carbon dioxide levels didn&rsquo;t increase at important periods in sauropod evolution, not that higher levels would have affected that evolution. The text provides no information about how higher levels of atmospheric carbon dioxide might have affected sauropods.&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-007-INF$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$f0030868-1f3e-4b1c-83bb-7be8d30399ed$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"123bd312","external_id":"d611db07-c76b-4fcd-97f2-12bb13024b07","vaultid":"dd8a1c0e-f827-42e5-9553-cd89efb18b68","uId":"4c92cb78-2c3f-4d81-8d2e-a06f691ba16e","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"INF","skill_desc":"Inferences","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Herbivorous sauropod dinosaurs could grow more than 100 feet long and weigh up to 80 tons, and some researchers have attributed the evolution of sauropods to such massive sizes to increased plant production resulting from high levels of atmospheric carbon dioxide during the Mesozoic era. However, there is no evidence of significant spikes in carbon dioxide levels coinciding with relevant periods in sauropod evolution, such as when the first large sauropods appeared, when several sauropod lineages underwent further evolution toward gigantism, or when sauropods reached their maximum known sizes, suggesting that <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span></p>","stem":"<p>Which choice most logically completes the text?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"c1ea4ee7-0cd4-4501-a3e2-1754ea4b7a74","content":"<p>fluctuations in atmospheric carbon dioxide affected different sauropod lineages differently.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"682dd2cc-756e-4c1e-ab6b-7a8ffd00a810","content":"<p>the evolution of larger body sizes in sauropods did not depend on increased atmospheric carbon dioxide.</p>"},{"id":"70b25aec-6644-4c28-bfbd-b7b817ad24fe","content":"<p>atmospheric carbon dioxide was higher when the largest known sauropods lived than it was when the first sauropods appeared.</p>"},{"id":"d17fc41d-2023-483f-88a0-ad7f35e7eb86","content":"<p>sauropods probably would not have evolved to such immense sizes if atmospheric carbon dioxide had been even slightly higher.</p>"}],"keys":["682dd2cc-756e-4c1e-ab6b-7a8ffd00a810"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer because it presents the conclusion that most logically follows from the text&rsquo;s discussion of the relationship between atmospheric carbon dioxide and sauropod body size. The text establishes that sauropods evolved to reach enormous sizes, and it notes that some scientists have asserted that the cause of this phenomenon was increased plant production that resulted from increased atmospheric carbon dioxide. The text goes on to state, however, that atmospheric carbon dioxide levels didn&rsquo;t increase around the time of important periods in sauropods&rsquo; evolution of larger body sizes. If significant periods of sauropod evolution toward larger sizes occurred without increased atmospheric carbon dioxide levels, that suggests that the evolution of larger sizes didn&rsquo;t depend on increased carbon dioxide in the atmosphere.&nbsp;</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t describe any fluctuations in atmospheric carbon dioxide, so there&rsquo;s no evidence in the text to support the conclusion that such fluctuations had different effects on different sauropod lineages. All that the text says about atmospheric carbon dioxide levels is that there weren&rsquo;t increases at particular points that correspond with key moments in sauropod evolution.&nbsp;Choice C is incorrect because the text indicates that there weren&rsquo;t significant increases in atmospheric carbon dioxide around the time of important periods in sauropods&rsquo; evolution toward larger body sizes, not that atmospheric carbon dioxide was higher when the largest sauropods lived than when sauropods first appeared.&nbsp;Choice D is incorrect because the text indicates that atmospheric carbon dioxide levels didn&rsquo;t increase at important periods in sauropod evolution, not that higher levels would have affected that evolution. The text provides no information about how higher levels of atmospheric carbon dioxide might have affected sauropods.&nbsp;</p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-007-INF","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"f0030868-1f3e-4b1c-83bb-7be8d30399ed","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959313,"updateDate":1691007959313}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959313, 1691007959313),
    ($SATQ$124fdcd7$SATQ$, $SATQ$93d69a24-a340-41cd-a1f4-68f2d6b98e24$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$498a8f1b-9e0b-4fd1-b0f4-64eaf2adec37$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$COE$SATQ$, $SATQ$Command of Evidence$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Many archaeologists will tell you that categorizing excavated fragments of pottery by style, period, and what objects they belong to relies not only on standard criteria, but also on instinct developed over years of practice. In a recent study, however, researchers trained a deep-learning computer model on thousands of images of pottery fragments and found that it could categorize them as accurately as a team of expert archaeologists. Some archaeologists have expressed concern that they might be replaced by such computer models, but the researchers claim that outcome is highly unlikely.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which finding, if true, would most directly support the researchers&rsquo; claim?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"fbd918ff-c1ac-4f6a-9aff-4c19c21a6a65","content":"<p>In the researchers&rsquo; study, the model was able to categorize the pottery fragments much more quickly than the archaeologists could.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"9d4094b5-dd4b-45cc-b55e-f8098db2a19d","content":"<p>In the researchers&rsquo; study, neither the model nor the archeologists were able to accurately categorize all the pottery fragments that were presented.</p>"},{"id":"45a197c7-95ce-4171-a5eb-4fe0ea0ef3ff","content":"<p>A survey of archaeologists showed that categorizing pottery fragments limits the amount of time they can dedicate to other important tasks that only human experts can do.</p>"},{"id":"9216bace-776b-4381-beeb-36ec2e1d3545","content":"<p>A survey of archaeologists showed that few of them received dedicated training in how to properly categorize pottery fragments.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["45a197c7-95ce-4171-a5eb-4fe0ea0ef3ff"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer because it presents a finding that, if true, would support the researchers&rsquo; claim that archaeologists are unlikely to be replaced by certain computer models. The text explains that although archaeologists hold that categorizing pottery fragments relies on both objective criteria and instinct developed through direct experience, researchers have found that a computer model can categorize the fragments with the same degree of accuracy as the humans can&mdash;a finding that has caused some archaeologists to worry that their own work won&rsquo;t be needed any longer. If survey results indicate that categorizing pottery fragments limits the amount of time archaeologists can dedicate to other important tasks that only human experts can do, that would mean that computer models aren&rsquo;t able to do all of the important things archaeologists do, thus supporting the researchers&rsquo; claim that computer models are unlikely to replace human archaeologists. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because if it were true that the computer model could categorize the pottery fragments much more quickly than the archaeologists could, that would weaken the researchers&rsquo; claim that archaeologists are unlikely to be replaced by certain computer models, since it would demonstrate that the models could conduct the archaeologists&rsquo; work not only with equal accuracy but also at a faster pace. Choice B is incorrect because the inability of both the computer model and the archaeologists to accurately categorize all of the pottery fragments presented wouldn&rsquo;t support the researchers&rsquo; claim that archaeologists are unlikely to be replaced by certain computer models. The text indicates that some archaeologists are worried because the computer model&rsquo;s accuracy is equal to their own, and that could be the case whether both were perfectly accurate or were unable to achieve complete accuracy. Choice D is incorrect because survey results showing that few archaeologists received special training in properly categorizing pottery fragments wouldn&rsquo;t support the researchers&rsquo; claim that archaeologists are unlikely to be replaced by certain computer models. The amount of special training in categorizing pottery fragments that archaeologists have received has no direct bearing on whether computer models&rsquo; success at categorizing fragments will lead to the models replacing the archaeologists.&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-304-TXT$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$6d700bbd-9512-4732-998b-9fd4539e12ce$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"124fdcd7","external_id":"93d69a24-a340-41cd-a1f4-68f2d6b98e24","vaultid":"498a8f1b-9e0b-4fd1-b0f4-64eaf2adec37","uId":"fda44b70-feff-451b-bf47-979f2a01a783","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"COE","skill_desc":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Many archaeologists will tell you that categorizing excavated fragments of pottery by style, period, and what objects they belong to relies not only on standard criteria, but also on instinct developed over years of practice. In a recent study, however, researchers trained a deep-learning computer model on thousands of images of pottery fragments and found that it could categorize them as accurately as a team of expert archaeologists. Some archaeologists have expressed concern that they might be replaced by such computer models, but the researchers claim that outcome is highly unlikely.</p>","stem":"<p>Which finding, if true, would most directly support the researchers&rsquo; claim?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"fbd918ff-c1ac-4f6a-9aff-4c19c21a6a65","content":"<p>In the researchers&rsquo; study, the model was able to categorize the pottery fragments much more quickly than the archaeologists could.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"9d4094b5-dd4b-45cc-b55e-f8098db2a19d","content":"<p>In the researchers&rsquo; study, neither the model nor the archeologists were able to accurately categorize all the pottery fragments that were presented.</p>"},{"id":"45a197c7-95ce-4171-a5eb-4fe0ea0ef3ff","content":"<p>A survey of archaeologists showed that categorizing pottery fragments limits the amount of time they can dedicate to other important tasks that only human experts can do.</p>"},{"id":"9216bace-776b-4381-beeb-36ec2e1d3545","content":"<p>A survey of archaeologists showed that few of them received dedicated training in how to properly categorize pottery fragments.</p>"}],"keys":["45a197c7-95ce-4171-a5eb-4fe0ea0ef3ff"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer because it presents a finding that, if true, would support the researchers&rsquo; claim that archaeologists are unlikely to be replaced by certain computer models. The text explains that although archaeologists hold that categorizing pottery fragments relies on both objective criteria and instinct developed through direct experience, researchers have found that a computer model can categorize the fragments with the same degree of accuracy as the humans can&mdash;a finding that has caused some archaeologists to worry that their own work won&rsquo;t be needed any longer. If survey results indicate that categorizing pottery fragments limits the amount of time archaeologists can dedicate to other important tasks that only human experts can do, that would mean that computer models aren&rsquo;t able to do all of the important things archaeologists do, thus supporting the researchers&rsquo; claim that computer models are unlikely to replace human archaeologists. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because if it were true that the computer model could categorize the pottery fragments much more quickly than the archaeologists could, that would weaken the researchers&rsquo; claim that archaeologists are unlikely to be replaced by certain computer models, since it would demonstrate that the models could conduct the archaeologists&rsquo; work not only with equal accuracy but also at a faster pace. Choice B is incorrect because the inability of both the computer model and the archaeologists to accurately categorize all of the pottery fragments presented wouldn&rsquo;t support the researchers&rsquo; claim that archaeologists are unlikely to be replaced by certain computer models. The text indicates that some archaeologists are worried because the computer model&rsquo;s accuracy is equal to their own, and that could be the case whether both were perfectly accurate or were unable to achieve complete accuracy. Choice D is incorrect because survey results showing that few archaeologists received special training in properly categorizing pottery fragments wouldn&rsquo;t support the researchers&rsquo; claim that archaeologists are unlikely to be replaced by certain computer models. The amount of special training in categorizing pottery fragments that archaeologists have received has no direct bearing on whether computer models&rsquo; success at categorizing fragments will lead to the models replacing the archaeologists.&nbsp;</p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-304-TXT","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"6d700bbd-9512-4732-998b-9fd4539e12ce","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959334,"updateDate":1691007959334}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959334, 1691007959334),
    ($SATQ$1281dfd5$SATQ$, $SATQ$a9859e96-a39d-4207-8cdb-6fb0ee8d8a98$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$COE$SATQ$, $SATQ$Command of Evidence$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<figure class="image"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="400" height="440.11796951293945" viewBox="0 0 400 440.11796951293945" role="img" aria-label="Bar graph titled Number of Lizard Species by Average Percent of Maximal Speed Used When Pursuing Prey or Escaping Predators. The horizontal axis is labeled Percent of maximal speed. 7 data categories are shown. The vertical axis is labeled Number of lizard species. It ranges from 0 to 9 in increments of 1. Refer to long description."><g id="ed420550-79eb-48d4-af01-cd27cdd08afd" data-name="Layer 1"><defs>
