e-width="0.9"/><text xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" x="0" y="0" text-anchor="end" transform="translate(323.31067762548287 315.84) rotate(-40)" fill="#000000" font-size="19.84" font-family="Crimson Text">80&ndash;89</text><rect xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" x="342.0344065752898" y="118.22222222222223" height="177.77777777777777" width="14.321864474903453" fill="#B3B3B3" stroke="#000000" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="mitre" stroke-width="0.9"/><rect xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" x="356.35627105019324" y="229.33333333333334" height="66.66666666666666" width="14.321864474903453" fill="#333333" stroke="#000000" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="mitre" stroke-width="0.9"/><text xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" x="0" y="0" text-anchor="end" transform="translate(366.27627105019326 315.84) rotate(-40)" fill="#000000" font-size="19.84" font-family="Crimson Text">90&ndash;100</text><text text-anchor="middle" transform="translate(227.459490776062 24)" fill="#000000" font-size="19.84" font-family="Crimson Text">Number of Lizard Species by Average </text><text text-anchor="middle" transform="translate(227.459490776062 48)" fill="#000000" font-size="19.84" font-family="Crimson Text">Percent of Maximal Speed Used When </text><text text-anchor="middle" transform="translate(227.459490776062 72)" fill="#000000" font-size="19.84" font-family="Crimson Text">Pursuing Prey or Escaping Predators</text><text text-anchor="middle" transform="translate(227.459490776062 385.117961730957)" fill="#000000" font-size="19.84" font-family="Crimson Text">Percent of maximal speed </text><rect xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" fill="none" x="120.72218227386475" y="402.27796173095703" height="26.84" width="213.47461700439453" stroke="#000000" stroke-linejoin="mitre" stroke-width="0.9"/><rect xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" x="127.72218227386475" y="409.27796173095703" height="12" width="12" fill="#B3B3B3" stroke="#000000" stroke-linejoin="mitre" stroke-width="0.9"/><text transform="translate(146.72218227386475 421.27796173095703)" text-anchor="top" fill="#000000" font-size="19.84" font-family="Crimson Text">escaping</text><rect xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" x="230.57458400726318" y="409.27796173095703" height="12" width="12" fill="#333333" stroke="#000000" stroke-linejoin="mitre" stroke-width="0.9"/><text transform="translate(249.57458400726318 421.27796173095703)" text-anchor="top" fill="#000000" font-size="19.84" font-family="Crimson Text">pursuing</text></g></svg></figure><div class="sr-only" role="region" aria-label="Long description for bar graph titled Number of Lizard Species by Average Percent of Maximal Speed Used When Pursuing Prey or Escaping Predators"><ul>
<li>For each data category, the following bars are shown:<br/>
<ul>
<li>escaping</li>
<li>pursuing</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li>The data for the 7 categories are as follows:<br/>
<ul>
<li>30&ndash;39 percent of maximal speed:<br/>
<ul>
<li>escaping: 0 species</li>
<li>pursuing: 1 species</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li>40&ndash;49 percent of maximal speed:<br/>
<ul>
<li>escaping: 0 species</li>
<li>pursuing: 0 species</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li>50&ndash;59 percent of maximal speed:<br/>
<ul>
<li>escaping: 2 species</li>
<li>pursuing: 1 species</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li>60&ndash;69 percent of maximal speed:<br/>
<ul>
<li>escaping: 1 species</li>
<li>pursuing: 1 species</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li>70&ndash;79 percent of maximal speed:<br/>
<ul>
<li>escaping: 2 species</li>
<li>pursuing: 0 species</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li>80&ndash;89 percent of maximal speed:<br/>
<ul>
<li>escaping: 3 species</li>
<li>pursuing: 1 species</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li>90&ndash;100 percent of maximal speed:<br/>
<ul>
<li>escaping: 8 species</li>
<li>pursuing: 3 species</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul></div>
<p>It may seem that the optimal strategy for an animal pursuing prey or escaping predators is to move at maximal speed, but the energy expense of exploiting full speed capacity can disfavor such a strategy even in escape contexts, as evidenced by the fact that <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span></p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice most effectively uses data from the graph to complete the text?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"dd95c9aa-658e-4a33-a0f1-5acb106801aa","content":"<p>most lizard species use about the same percentage of their maximal speed when escaping predation as they do when pursuing prey.</p>"},{"id":"64c8ead7-c0a1-4dd8-9375-f4595b979231","content":"<p>multiple lizard species move at an average of less than 90% of their maximal speed while escaping predation.</p>"},{"id":"b2d393e5-911e-4a05-8adb-4e7949048c13","content":"<p>more lizard species use, on average, 90%&ndash;100% of their maximal speed while escaping predation than use any other percentage of their maximal speed.</p>"},{"id":"ac6ab2c6-a249-40cd-b848-fa37a6bc5570","content":"<p>at least 4 lizard species use, on average, less than 100% of their maximal speed while pursuing prey.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["64c8ead7-c0a1-4dd8-9375-f4595b979231"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer because it describes data from the graph that complete the text&rsquo;s discussion of lizard species&rsquo; use of maximal speed when escaping predators. According to the text, moving at maximal speed (the highest speed possible) requires so much energy that it is not always an effective strategy for animals, even when they are escaping predators. The graph displays data on the average percent of maximal speed used by lizard species while either escaping predators or pursuing prey. The graph categorizes the data for both pursuing and escaping by the number of species using 30%&ndash;39% of maximal speed, 40%&ndash;49% of maximal speed, 50%&ndash;59% of maximal speed, 60%&ndash;69% of maximal speed, 70%&ndash;79% of maximal speed, 80%&ndash;89% of maximal speed, and 90%&ndash;100% of maximal speed, respectively. In the graph, there is at least one species in each of the following percent categories for maximal speed while escaping predators: 50%&ndash;59%, 60%&ndash;69%, 70%&ndash;79%, and 80%&ndash;89%. Thus, the data in the graph show that multiple lizard species move at an average of less than 90% of their maximal speed while escaping predation.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the data in the graph isn&rsquo;t organized in such a way that a comparison of the percentage of maximal speed used when escaping predation with the percentage used when pursuing prey is possible at the level of individual species. Choice C is incorrect. It is true that in the graph, the percent category with the largest number of species using maximal speed while escaping predators is 90%&ndash;100% (8 species total). However, these data don&rsquo;t complete the text, which is concerned instead with how animals are discouraged from using maximal speed even when escaping predators because of the amount of energy required to use it. Choice D is incorrect because these data from the graph pertain to maximal speed while pursuing prey and therefore don&rsquo;t complete the text&rsquo;s discussion of lizard species&rsquo; use of maximal speed when escaping predators.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"1281dfd5","external_id":"a9859e96-a39d-4207-8cdb-6fb0ee8d8a98","vaultid":null,"uId":"0a50df90-a7de-4787-8c71-fe936b72e62b","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"COE","skill_desc":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<figure class=\"image\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"400\" height=\"440.11796951293945\" viewBox=\"0 0 